WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:15.170 --> 00:00:16.860
TARYN MURPHY: My
name is Taryn Murphy.

00:00:16.860 --> 00:00:18.570
I'm a junior here
at the college.

00:00:18.570 --> 00:00:21.160
And I'm studying
philosophy and religion.

00:00:21.160 --> 00:00:23.390
And I'm very honored to
introduce our speaker

00:00:23.390 --> 00:00:25.400
this evening.

00:00:25.400 --> 00:00:29.000
Kimberley Strassel writes the
weekly Potomac Watch column

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:30.980
for The Wall Street
Journal, where

00:00:30.980 --> 00:00:34.780
she is also a member
of the editorial board.

00:00:34.780 --> 00:00:37.280
A graduate of
Princeton University,

00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:39.680
her previous positions
at the Journal

00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:42.770
include news
assistant in Brussels,

00:00:42.770 --> 00:00:47.150
internet reporter in London,
commercial real estate reporter

00:00:47.150 --> 00:00:51.755
in New York, columnist
for OpinionJournal.com,

00:00:51.755 --> 00:00:55.390
and senior editorial
page writer.

00:00:55.390 --> 00:00:57.890
She is a regular contributor
to Sunday morning

00:00:57.890 --> 00:01:01.760
political programs,
including Face the Nation

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:04.450
and Meet the Press.

00:01:04.450 --> 00:01:09.160
In 2013, she was a Eugene
C. Pulliam Distinguished

00:01:09.160 --> 00:01:13.450
Visiting Fellow in Journalism
at Hillsdale College.

00:01:13.450 --> 00:01:19.090
And in 2014, she was a
recipient of the Bradley Prize.

00:01:19.090 --> 00:01:22.900
She is the author of
The Intimidation Game--

00:01:22.900 --> 00:01:28.030
How the Left is Silencing Free
Speech, and most recently,

00:01:28.030 --> 00:01:31.210
Resistance At All Costs--

00:01:31.210 --> 00:01:34.630
How Trump Haters are
Breaking America.

00:01:34.630 --> 00:01:36.400
Please help me welcome
Kimberley Strassel.

00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:38.664
[APPLAUSE]

00:01:51.430 --> 00:01:54.010
KIMBERLEY STRASSEL:
Good evening.

00:01:54.010 --> 00:01:56.770
It is honestly such
a joy to be here.

00:01:56.770 --> 00:02:01.160
Every time I get to come
to Hillsdale, it's a joy.

00:02:01.160 --> 00:02:04.060
I want to thank
Hillsdale and the Center

00:02:04.060 --> 00:02:08.110
for Constructive Alternatives
for having me here tonight.

00:02:08.110 --> 00:02:11.470
Just driving in last
night, every time I come,

00:02:11.470 --> 00:02:15.280
the growth and the amazing
changes at this place--

00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:16.690
we drove in last night.

00:02:16.690 --> 00:02:18.340
And I said to my
husband, I could

00:02:18.340 --> 00:02:23.440
swear that enormous chapel was
not there when we were just

00:02:23.440 --> 00:02:26.060
here a couple of years ago.

00:02:26.060 --> 00:02:27.590
It's astonishing.

00:02:27.590 --> 00:02:30.670
My one small complaint--
it was funny.

00:02:30.670 --> 00:02:33.340
Last night-- and
my bad, by the way,

00:02:33.340 --> 00:02:36.640
too, because I changed my
mind about this in the end.

00:02:36.640 --> 00:02:39.340
As we were driving
here, all these miles,

00:02:39.340 --> 00:02:41.860
last night, I had this thought.

00:02:41.860 --> 00:02:45.430
And it's the thought that all
overbusy, overbooked people

00:02:45.430 --> 00:02:48.610
have when they're just trying
to get to a place, which

00:02:48.610 --> 00:02:53.890
is, why does a college that is
so important to free speech,

00:02:53.890 --> 00:02:57.400
and to intellectual exchange,
and so important to reminding

00:02:57.400 --> 00:03:00.100
us about the history of the
country and our founding

00:03:00.100 --> 00:03:03.480
principles have to be located
in the middle of nowhere?

00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:05.440
[LAUGHTER]

00:03:06.910 --> 00:03:08.870
[APPLAUSE]

00:03:11.320 --> 00:03:15.200
And then I got here and
eased into Hillsdale,

00:03:15.200 --> 00:03:17.160
and had a glass of wine.

00:03:17.160 --> 00:03:19.890
And, of course, the
very obvious point

00:03:19.890 --> 00:03:22.980
hit me, which is that,
of course, the reason

00:03:22.980 --> 00:03:26.940
that Hillsdale has remained
so committed to free speech,

00:03:26.940 --> 00:03:30.060
and exchange, and to the history
and values of our country

00:03:30.060 --> 00:03:33.360
is because it is
located so far away

00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:36.750
from every other population
center, all of which

00:03:36.750 --> 00:03:38.590
have abandoned those values.

00:03:38.590 --> 00:03:41.760
So another way of saying,
well done, and please

00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:43.920
don't ever move anywhere
else in the country.

00:03:46.880 --> 00:03:51.310
I think Hillsdale may, in
fact, be the perfect venue

00:03:51.310 --> 00:03:54.460
to address a question
of why we are

00:03:54.460 --> 00:03:57.370
seeing a resurgence
of socialism today.

00:03:57.370 --> 00:04:00.450
And bear with me
while I explain that.

00:04:00.450 --> 00:04:04.630
When we talk about the rise of
the popularity of socialism,

00:04:04.630 --> 00:04:08.230
the polls show that it is
mostly younger Americans who

00:04:08.230 --> 00:04:10.130
are embracing it.

00:04:10.130 --> 00:04:14.740
This is a problem primarily,
albeit not exclusively,

00:04:14.740 --> 00:04:19.000
of youth, which, by the way,
isn't necessarily a surprise.

00:04:19.000 --> 00:04:21.260
It's kind of like
that old story.

00:04:21.260 --> 00:04:25.880
There's a child who says to
their parent, when I grow up,

00:04:25.880 --> 00:04:28.000
I want to be a socialist.

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:31.930
And the parent replies, OK, but
the problem with that, honey,

00:04:31.930 --> 00:04:34.550
is that you know that
you can't do both.

00:04:34.550 --> 00:04:37.015
[LAUGHTER]

00:04:37.015 --> 00:04:38.987
[APPLAUSE]

00:04:43.930 --> 00:04:47.580
But the thing is, writing
this off as an issue of youth

00:04:47.580 --> 00:04:51.330
isn't good enough because,
obviously, socialism

00:04:51.330 --> 00:04:54.960
isn't an innate issue to youth.

00:04:54.960 --> 00:04:57.150
It's difficult, for
instance, to imagine

00:04:57.150 --> 00:05:01.440
a socialist philosophy sweeping
a campus like Hillsdale.

00:05:01.440 --> 00:05:04.560
This is an institution committed
to providing its students

00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:10.140
with facts, history, economics,
the truth of the founding

00:05:10.140 --> 00:05:13.860
of this country, and the
reasons for its success,

00:05:13.860 --> 00:05:17.580
as well as other examples
of other countries

00:05:17.580 --> 00:05:20.560
that have not fared as well.

00:05:20.560 --> 00:05:23.880
And all of this has allowed
even the youth of this campus

00:05:23.880 --> 00:05:26.790
to obtain a realistic
understanding

00:05:26.790 --> 00:05:29.920
of the dangers of socialism.

00:05:29.920 --> 00:05:32.400
It's also not good enough,
though, to just sit back

00:05:32.400 --> 00:05:35.260
and to assume that the rest
of the country's youth,

00:05:35.260 --> 00:05:40.330
those outside of Hillsdale,
will inevitably grow up.

00:05:40.330 --> 00:05:43.990
The problem is just too urgent
right now in the country.

00:05:43.990 --> 00:05:47.820
There are literally millions
of younger Americans who

00:05:47.820 --> 00:05:51.360
today either openly declare
themselves socialists

00:05:51.360 --> 00:05:55.980
or say that they have favorable
views of that philosophy.

00:05:55.980 --> 00:05:58.560
Those young Americans,
in turn, have the ability

00:05:58.560 --> 00:06:02.190
to vote, and to
vote, ironically,

00:06:02.190 --> 00:06:06.570
for much older politicians
who should know better,

00:06:06.570 --> 00:06:08.730
who should know their history.

00:06:08.730 --> 00:06:12.350
And those politicians,
in turn, if given power,

00:06:12.350 --> 00:06:14.910
are promising policies
that would radically

00:06:14.910 --> 00:06:17.250
undermine the system
of capitalism that

00:06:17.250 --> 00:06:19.620
has served as the
strength of this nation

00:06:19.620 --> 00:06:22.260
since its beginning,
which leads us

00:06:22.260 --> 00:06:24.210
to another old
saying and something

00:06:24.210 --> 00:06:28.380
that Dr. Arnn alluded to
earlier, the joke about--

00:06:28.380 --> 00:06:32.670
any joke about socialism is only
funny when everybody gets it.

00:06:32.670 --> 00:06:34.083
[LAUGHTER]

00:06:35.315 --> 00:06:36.690
If we want to know
how to fix it,

00:06:36.690 --> 00:06:40.150
though, we first have to
understand the root causes.

00:06:40.150 --> 00:06:43.710
And we've had many capable
academics, and sociologists,

00:06:43.710 --> 00:06:45.960
and political
observers now document

00:06:45.960 --> 00:06:49.530
some of the structural
shifts that have arguably

00:06:49.530 --> 00:06:52.170
contributed to this new
interest in a very, very

00:06:52.170 --> 00:06:54.450
dangerous philosophy.

00:06:54.450 --> 00:06:58.950
Globalization has led to greater
levels of wealth inequality

00:06:58.950 --> 00:07:01.650
and a new politics of envy.

00:07:01.650 --> 00:07:05.580
That globalization, coupled with
overregulation in the United

00:07:05.580 --> 00:07:10.050
States and at times poorly
managed trade policy,

00:07:10.050 --> 00:07:13.590
has displaced entire US
manufacturing sectors

00:07:13.590 --> 00:07:17.670
and hollowed out significant
swaths of the middle class.

00:07:17.670 --> 00:07:20.640
Ever-growing government,
alongside schemes

00:07:20.640 --> 00:07:24.990
like gerrymandering, has
sheltered too many politicians

00:07:24.990 --> 00:07:27.960
from accountability and left
millions of Americans feeling

00:07:27.960 --> 00:07:30.270
left behind and
more importantly,

00:07:30.270 --> 00:07:35.310
alienated from what they see
as an elitist ruling class.

00:07:35.310 --> 00:07:37.440
And then we've had
huge cultural shifts

00:07:37.440 --> 00:07:39.610
as well too in this country.

00:07:39.610 --> 00:07:41.760
Those younger Americans
we were talking about,

00:07:41.760 --> 00:07:44.860
they aren't growing up.

00:07:44.860 --> 00:07:46.930
They aren't being
asked to grow up.

00:07:46.930 --> 00:07:50.600
They are living at
home until their 30s.

00:07:50.600 --> 00:07:53.200
They're getting married
later, putting off children,

00:07:53.200 --> 00:07:55.900
putting off buying a
home, all the things

00:07:55.900 --> 00:07:58.300
that usually lead
developing adults

00:07:58.300 --> 00:08:01.180
to be more skeptical
of government schemes

00:08:01.180 --> 00:08:03.400
and government plans
for redistribution.

00:08:03.400 --> 00:08:06.580
By the way, I should just say,
in my house, one of the mottoes

00:08:06.580 --> 00:08:08.140
is and I say to
all my children--

00:08:08.140 --> 00:08:10.180
they are now 14, 12, and eight.

00:08:10.180 --> 00:08:12.490
And I say, there
is only one thing

00:08:12.490 --> 00:08:16.670
I can promise will happen to
you when you turn 18 years old

00:08:16.670 --> 00:08:19.720
and that is that your room
is going to be repurposed.

00:08:19.720 --> 00:08:21.580
[LAUGHTER]

00:08:21.580 --> 00:08:23.440
[APPLAUSE]

00:08:26.240 --> 00:08:29.630
But those kids out there
who do dare to leave home

00:08:29.630 --> 00:08:31.430
are more and more--
they're funneled

00:08:31.430 --> 00:08:33.620
into dead-end
degrees, which lead

00:08:33.620 --> 00:08:36.320
to dead-end jobs in
urban areas where it's

00:08:36.320 --> 00:08:39.700
impossible to get ahead,
impossible to buy a home,

00:08:39.700 --> 00:08:42.230
and where they are,
again, surrounded daily

00:08:42.230 --> 00:08:45.680
by woke liberal politics
and policies that

00:08:45.680 --> 00:08:50.040
are designed to pit
American against American.

00:08:50.040 --> 00:08:53.560
Now, these shifts are very
real and very important.

00:08:53.560 --> 00:08:58.620
But even identifying them is not
good enough because even big,

00:08:58.620 --> 00:09:02.950
cosmic structural shifts
do not happen in a vacuum.

00:09:02.950 --> 00:09:06.100
There are things
behind them as well.

00:09:06.100 --> 00:09:08.940
They are the result of
conscious decisions.

00:09:08.940 --> 00:09:11.440
Trade doesn't just happen.

00:09:11.440 --> 00:09:12.870
It's negotiated.

00:09:12.870 --> 00:09:15.340
Regulation doesn't just happen.

00:09:15.340 --> 00:09:16.860
It's crafted.

00:09:16.860 --> 00:09:20.530
Political unaccountability
doesn't just happen.

00:09:20.530 --> 00:09:22.410
It is deliberate.

00:09:22.410 --> 00:09:27.270
No one is making kids stay at
home until they're middle-aged.

00:09:27.270 --> 00:09:29.490
The decision to do
so is a response

00:09:29.490 --> 00:09:32.370
to job opportunities,
housing policies,

00:09:32.370 --> 00:09:35.610
poor or misguided
education, and rules

00:09:35.610 --> 00:09:39.000
that allow their parents to
continue funding their health

00:09:39.000 --> 00:09:42.600
care until they
are 26 years old.

00:09:42.600 --> 00:09:47.040
And so we can't just blame
youth for not knowing better.

00:09:47.040 --> 00:09:48.940
It's like Dr. Arnn said earlier.

00:09:48.940 --> 00:09:50.680
They're learning.

00:09:50.680 --> 00:09:54.190
And they are the product
of parenting, of education,

00:09:54.190 --> 00:09:58.110
of political policy,
and of culture.

00:09:58.110 --> 00:10:00.450
So instead, I think that
we have to acknowledge

00:10:00.450 --> 00:10:04.170
that these shifts and
that the subsequent rise

00:10:04.170 --> 00:10:06.420
in the popularity
of socialism are,

00:10:06.420 --> 00:10:10.650
in fact, the result of failures
of core groups of officials

00:10:10.650 --> 00:10:12.720
in civic institutions.

00:10:12.720 --> 00:10:15.240
And tonight, I'd like to
make the case that there

00:10:15.240 --> 00:10:19.740
are four in particular that we
need to talk about and put out

00:10:19.740 --> 00:10:21.270
there for blame.

00:10:21.270 --> 00:10:27.270
They are the Democratic
Party, the Republican Party,

00:10:27.270 --> 00:10:30.570
the media, and our
system of education,

00:10:30.570 --> 00:10:33.270
in particular,
higher education--

00:10:33.270 --> 00:10:36.390
Hillsdale, of course, excluded.

00:10:36.390 --> 00:10:39.400
So let's run through them.

00:10:39.400 --> 00:10:41.790
Let's look at the
Democratic Party,

00:10:41.790 --> 00:10:43.740
some of its most
influential members

00:10:43.740 --> 00:10:47.280
of which have adopted an
outright socialist agenda--

00:10:47.280 --> 00:10:50.030
Bernie Sanders,
Elizabeth Warren,

00:10:50.030 --> 00:10:51.960
Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez--

00:10:51.960 --> 00:10:55.350
I'm always so proud when
I can say that name--

00:10:55.350 --> 00:10:58.290
her entire squad.

00:10:58.290 --> 00:11:01.590
But it's a lot more broad
than just those names.

00:11:01.590 --> 00:11:03.930
As I point out in the
book I just put out,

00:11:03.930 --> 00:11:06.630
this is the most
socialist Congress

00:11:06.630 --> 00:11:09.120
in the history of this country.

00:11:09.120 --> 00:11:11.970
Following the 2018 congressional
election-- some of you

00:11:11.970 --> 00:11:15.330
may know Nate Silver's
FiveThirtyEight blog.

00:11:15.330 --> 00:11:17.610
And it pointed out
that in 2010, there

00:11:17.610 --> 00:11:21.570
were about 1.5 Progressives
or liberal members

00:11:21.570 --> 00:11:26.130
of Congress for every blue
dog moderate in the House.

00:11:26.130 --> 00:11:29.850
Right now, post
the 2018 election,

00:11:29.850 --> 00:11:32.130
the Progressives currently
outweigh the moderates

00:11:32.130 --> 00:11:35.760
in the party four to one.

00:11:35.760 --> 00:11:40.290
And just a quick note I think
we should make about terms.

00:11:40.290 --> 00:11:45.880
Progressivism, let me say
right now, is socialism.

00:11:45.880 --> 00:11:47.890
Progressivism is
that new word that

00:11:47.890 --> 00:11:51.980
just burst into the political
ideology in recent years.

00:11:51.980 --> 00:11:55.390
But the question is, why
did we need a new word?

00:11:55.390 --> 00:11:57.817
The Democratic Party's
always had liberals.

00:11:57.817 --> 00:11:58.900
It's always had moderates.

00:11:58.900 --> 00:12:01.900
And that has always adequately
described the party and where

00:12:01.900 --> 00:12:04.630
it stood, and its splits.

00:12:04.630 --> 00:12:08.440
We have this term today
because there was a recognition

00:12:08.440 --> 00:12:11.500
just a few years ago on the
left that they needed a term

00:12:11.500 --> 00:12:13.810
to describe a
philosophy that went far

00:12:13.810 --> 00:12:17.230
beyond the traditional
liberal views of higher

00:12:17.230 --> 00:12:20.800
taxes, and bigger government,
and creative judges.

00:12:20.800 --> 00:12:23.580
And what is that philosophy?

00:12:23.580 --> 00:12:26.550
I would say, look at the plans
being advocated by Bernie

00:12:26.550 --> 00:12:29.190
Sanders and Elizabeth Warren.

00:12:29.190 --> 00:12:31.980
They would, in core
sectors of the economy,

00:12:31.980 --> 00:12:36.460
eradicate capitalism and replace
it with government-run tyranny,

00:12:36.460 --> 00:12:39.720
health care being
the best example.

00:12:39.720 --> 00:12:43.560
As for other sectors, their
plans would so radically

00:12:43.560 --> 00:12:46.660
regulate core sectors of
the economy-- finance,

00:12:46.660 --> 00:12:49.710
transportation, energy,
to name just a few--

00:12:49.710 --> 00:12:53.110
as to essentially make them
arms of the government.

00:12:53.110 --> 00:12:57.060
And they would also so tax all
of these sectors and businesses

00:12:57.060 --> 00:13:02.070
to such a degree that they
would cease to function

00:13:02.070 --> 00:13:03.780
on the behalf of shareholders.

00:13:03.780 --> 00:13:07.200
They would instead exist
as the central means

00:13:07.200 --> 00:13:10.230
by which the Sanders and
Warrens and AOCs of the world

00:13:10.230 --> 00:13:13.815
would redistribute
wealth in this country.

00:13:13.815 --> 00:13:16.120
Is everyone in this room--

00:13:16.120 --> 00:13:17.865
I'm sure most of
you are aware of it,

00:13:17.865 --> 00:13:21.495
of that famous description of
political and economic systems

00:13:21.495 --> 00:13:24.093
that's based on two cows?

00:13:24.093 --> 00:13:25.760
Some of you have
probably heard of this.

00:13:25.760 --> 00:13:28.180
So feudalism--
you have two cows,

00:13:28.180 --> 00:13:30.880
and your lord takes
some of the milk.

00:13:30.880 --> 00:13:34.690
Fascism-- you have two cows, the
government takes both of them,

00:13:34.690 --> 00:13:38.720
and hires you to take care of
them, and sells you the milk.

00:13:38.720 --> 00:13:42.610
A dictatorship-- you have two
cows, government takes both,

00:13:42.610 --> 00:13:43.630
and then it shoots you.

00:13:43.630 --> 00:13:46.090
[LAUGHTER]

00:13:46.090 --> 00:13:49.300
My favorite was always British
democracy-- you have two cows,

00:13:49.300 --> 00:13:50.860
and they both go mad.

00:13:50.860 --> 00:13:52.780
[LAUGHTER]

00:13:54.700 --> 00:13:58.840
American capitalism-- you
have two cows, you sell one,

00:13:58.840 --> 00:14:01.750
you buy a bull, and you grow
a herd, and you hire workers,

00:14:01.750 --> 00:14:04.270
and then you go public, and
then you get on the Forbes 500.

00:14:04.270 --> 00:14:06.034
[LAUGHTER]

00:14:06.920 --> 00:14:10.630
Socialism-- you have
three cows, government

00:14:10.630 --> 00:14:13.510
takes two, and gives them to
other people on the grounds

00:14:13.510 --> 00:14:16.300
that that makes things fair.

00:14:16.300 --> 00:14:20.350
This is pretty much the approach
of most of today's Progressives

00:14:20.350 --> 00:14:22.090
in the Democratic Party.

00:14:22.090 --> 00:14:25.840
They would take the majority
of other people's wealth,

00:14:25.840 --> 00:14:28.870
the majority of other people's
earnings, the majority

00:14:28.870 --> 00:14:32.320
of other people's property
to pay for free health care,

00:14:32.320 --> 00:14:36.490
for free education, for free
child care, for free drugs.

00:14:36.490 --> 00:14:38.980
So please don't be
fooled by Bernie Sanders'

00:14:38.980 --> 00:14:41.620
tortured attempts to
make a distinction

00:14:41.620 --> 00:14:46.040
between democratic socialism
and pure socialism.

00:14:46.040 --> 00:14:49.810
Socialism has long
predated even communism.

00:14:49.810 --> 00:14:53.450
And, in fact, it's always
existed in a variety of forms.

00:14:53.450 --> 00:14:56.680
If you look at Marx and Engels,
in their "Communist Manifesto,"

00:14:56.680 --> 00:15:00.040
they actually devoted an
entire section to criticizing

00:15:00.040 --> 00:15:03.580
the forms of socialism
that existed at the time

00:15:03.580 --> 00:15:05.770
that were not pure
enough for them.

00:15:05.770 --> 00:15:08.440
What they didn't like is
that there have always

00:15:08.440 --> 00:15:12.400
been socialists who believed in
some aspects of the market, who

00:15:12.400 --> 00:15:14.770
believed in some
property rights, who

00:15:14.770 --> 00:15:17.650
believed that people should be
able to keep a bit of what they

00:15:17.650 --> 00:15:19.420
make.

00:15:19.420 --> 00:15:21.460
Those who have attended
here the past few days

00:15:21.460 --> 00:15:23.440
have heard some
wonderful explanations

00:15:23.440 --> 00:15:27.290
of the different models of
socialism around the world.

00:15:27.290 --> 00:15:28.240
And so let's be clear.

00:15:28.240 --> 00:15:31.120
What Progressives
are advocating today

00:15:31.120 --> 00:15:34.180
is just another
version of socialism.

00:15:34.180 --> 00:15:38.990
It'd be a US version, sure,
but it's still socialism.

00:15:38.990 --> 00:15:41.680
It's a version in which
some markets would actually

00:15:41.680 --> 00:15:44.860
be allowed to exist, in
which some Americans,

00:15:44.860 --> 00:15:46.780
and corporations,
and wealthier people

00:15:46.780 --> 00:15:49.810
would be allowed to keep
some of what they earn.

00:15:49.810 --> 00:15:53.080
But they would, to be
clear, by and large

00:15:53.080 --> 00:15:56.440
be working for the
benefit of everyone else.

00:15:56.440 --> 00:15:58.380
That is socialism.

00:15:58.380 --> 00:16:01.150
And the only reason the
adherence to this philosophy--

00:16:01.150 --> 00:16:05.230
do not use that word openly
beyond Bernie Sanders--

00:16:05.230 --> 00:16:08.830
is because they know that
it remains a detestable idea

00:16:08.830 --> 00:16:10.690
for most Americans.

00:16:10.690 --> 00:16:13.960
So instead, we get the
word progressivism,

00:16:13.960 --> 00:16:16.060
or we hear Elizabeth
Warren actually

00:16:16.060 --> 00:16:20.530
say with a straight face
that she is a capitalist.

00:16:20.530 --> 00:16:22.300
She can say what she wants.

00:16:22.300 --> 00:16:25.810
Progressivism is socialism.

00:16:25.810 --> 00:16:28.270
So the bigger
question is, why have

00:16:28.270 --> 00:16:33.670
so many in the Democratic
Party moved left so quickly?

00:16:33.670 --> 00:16:37.030
Younger people in this country
who are adopting socialism

00:16:37.030 --> 00:16:41.540
at least have the excuse of
being ignorant of history.

00:16:41.540 --> 00:16:43.930
They are seemingly
unaware that if we

00:16:43.930 --> 00:16:47.980
in this room had $1 for every
time socialism was successful,

00:16:47.980 --> 00:16:50.710
we'd have $0.

00:16:50.710 --> 00:16:53.260
Of course, what's more notable
is that even if socialism did

00:16:53.260 --> 00:16:55.210
work, we'd all have $0, too.

00:16:55.210 --> 00:16:56.599
[LAUGHTER]

00:16:57.990 --> 00:17:01.870
They are also, unfortunately,
seemingly unaware--

00:17:01.870 --> 00:17:05.200
and more importantly-- of
the extraordinary cruelty

00:17:05.200 --> 00:17:09.579
and devastation that purer
forms of socialism have wrought.

00:17:09.579 --> 00:17:12.339
Socialism has, over
hundreds of years,

00:17:12.339 --> 00:17:16.329
meant the end of free speech,
free assembly, a free press,

00:17:16.329 --> 00:17:19.210
free religious belief,
free elections,

00:17:19.210 --> 00:17:21.280
and a free judiciary.

00:17:21.280 --> 00:17:22.900
Its more communistic
versions have

00:17:22.900 --> 00:17:26.410
resulted in imprisonment,
execution, starvation

00:17:26.410 --> 00:17:28.780
of tens of millions
of human beings.

00:17:28.780 --> 00:17:33.890
Look at the histories of Russia,
China, Korea, Cuba, Cambodia,

00:17:33.890 --> 00:17:37.960
Vietnam, Ethiopia, and
more recently, Venezuela.

00:17:37.960 --> 00:17:41.110
The list is long, and
it's horrifying, and right

00:17:41.110 --> 00:17:45.830
there in the history books in
black and white for all to see.

00:17:45.830 --> 00:17:49.940
So what excuse do older, wiser
members of the Democratic Party

00:17:49.940 --> 00:17:55.700
have for embracing such an
inexorably disastrous course?

00:17:55.700 --> 00:17:57.400
They know the history.

00:17:57.400 --> 00:17:59.780
And so I say, in
light of that, I

00:17:59.780 --> 00:18:04.530
think we have to adopt a more
cynical view of their motives.

00:18:04.530 --> 00:18:09.380
So I premise here that their
interest is not necessarily

00:18:09.380 --> 00:18:13.040
just equality and higher
standards of living

00:18:13.040 --> 00:18:15.710
or the betterment
of a free society

00:18:15.710 --> 00:18:17.930
because we all know
from the history books

00:18:17.930 --> 00:18:20.240
that that's not what you get.

00:18:20.240 --> 00:18:23.810
I would argue that their
motive, at least in part,

00:18:23.810 --> 00:18:25.700
is the same motive
that has propelled

00:18:25.700 --> 00:18:29.020
so many of the socialist
drives of the past.

00:18:29.020 --> 00:18:32.760
It's power, and it's control.

00:18:32.760 --> 00:18:34.410
Most parties, if
you think about it,

00:18:34.410 --> 00:18:37.530
when their message fails to
resonate with the public, when

00:18:37.530 --> 00:18:40.470
they lose, say, a
presidential election,

00:18:40.470 --> 00:18:43.920
they decide to take a step
back, lick their wounds,

00:18:43.920 --> 00:18:47.250
do an autopsy, figure
out what they did wrong,

00:18:47.250 --> 00:18:50.730
and come back the next
cycle and do it better.

00:18:50.730 --> 00:18:52.650
Unfortunately, this
has not been the habit

00:18:52.650 --> 00:18:55.770
of the Democratic Party
over the past 10 years.

00:18:55.770 --> 00:18:59.850
Political setbacks have not
been met with hard thought.

00:18:59.850 --> 00:19:02.340
They've instead been
met with an attitude of,

00:19:02.340 --> 00:19:04.810
how do we shackle
the other side,

00:19:04.810 --> 00:19:08.820
and how do we change the system
to make it easier to win?

00:19:08.820 --> 00:19:11.520
During the Obama years, we
had a new era, for instance,

00:19:11.520 --> 00:19:14.940
of intimidation, in which
democratic officials trained

00:19:14.940 --> 00:19:17.550
the federal bureaucracy
on their opponents,

00:19:17.550 --> 00:19:20.940
inspiring the IRS to target
conservative groups engaged

00:19:20.940 --> 00:19:22.740
in political activity.

00:19:22.740 --> 00:19:25.770
We had liberal prosecutors and
attorneys across the states

00:19:25.770 --> 00:19:29.150
launch criminal investigations
into their political opponents

00:19:29.150 --> 00:19:31.950
in Wisconsin, and
Montana, and New York.

00:19:31.950 --> 00:19:35.250
We've seen new speech
codes on campuses.

00:19:35.250 --> 00:19:38.910
And in the age of Trump,
this tendency has only grown.

00:19:38.910 --> 00:19:42.928
The attitude of the resistance,
again, the focus of my book--

00:19:42.928 --> 00:19:44.970
I'm just making a little
pitch here because we're

00:19:44.970 --> 00:19:46.350
a capitalistic audience--

00:19:46.350 --> 00:19:48.420
[LAUGHTER]

00:19:48.420 --> 00:19:51.030
--is that this president
and the Republicans

00:19:51.030 --> 00:19:54.150
are illegal occupiers of an
office, and that therefore,

00:19:54.150 --> 00:19:58.560
any action is justified
in removing them

00:19:58.560 --> 00:20:00.000
from that position.

00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:03.060
And so we've had an
unprecedented FBI

00:20:03.060 --> 00:20:06.420
counterintelligence
investigation into a sitting

00:20:06.420 --> 00:20:08.320
presidential campaign.

00:20:08.320 --> 00:20:10.530
Think about those words.

00:20:10.530 --> 00:20:13.080
We've had the most
raucous and ambush attack

00:20:13.080 --> 00:20:16.910
on a Supreme Court nominee
in history, Brett Kavanaugh.

00:20:16.910 --> 00:20:19.320
We have watched
House Democrats pass

00:20:19.320 --> 00:20:22.020
as their first
piece of legislation

00:20:22.020 --> 00:20:26.250
in the majority starting this
year a new voting reform that

00:20:26.250 --> 00:20:31.080
would pave the way for fraud at
every level at the ballot box.

00:20:31.080 --> 00:20:33.990
And we are right now
witnessing an unprecedented use

00:20:33.990 --> 00:20:38.100
of impeachment as a
partisan political tool.

00:20:38.100 --> 00:20:41.790
These are all extremely
dangerous new uses

00:20:41.790 --> 00:20:45.900
and abuses of what are meant
to be solemn powers exercised

00:20:45.900 --> 00:20:47.880
by solemn institutions.

00:20:47.880 --> 00:20:50.460
And they will potentially
have profound consequences

00:20:50.460 --> 00:20:53.460
for our democracy going forward.

00:20:53.460 --> 00:20:55.740
In the last election,
Hillary Clinton actually

00:20:55.740 --> 00:20:59.700
ran on promoting a
constitutional amendment that

00:20:59.700 --> 00:21:01.710
would have overturned
Citizens United

00:21:01.710 --> 00:21:03.810
and given politicians
the ability

00:21:03.810 --> 00:21:08.000
to define what was
appropriate political speech.

00:21:08.000 --> 00:21:10.300
Think about that idea.

00:21:10.300 --> 00:21:12.220
We have AOC--

00:21:12.220 --> 00:21:14.890
I'm not going to try
her name a second time--

00:21:14.890 --> 00:21:18.820
right now attempting to bully
Facebook into not running ads

00:21:18.820 --> 00:21:20.775
from one political party.

00:21:20.775 --> 00:21:23.920
And we have candidates
like Senator Warren calling

00:21:23.920 --> 00:21:27.280
for the abolition of
the electoral college,

00:21:27.280 --> 00:21:30.040
for the packing of
the Supreme Court.

00:21:30.040 --> 00:21:30.790
So let's be clear.

00:21:30.790 --> 00:21:33.910
Anyone who has even a
passing understanding

00:21:33.910 --> 00:21:36.520
of the glorious history
of our electoral college,

00:21:36.520 --> 00:21:39.550
of the beauty of that system
that our founders created,

00:21:39.550 --> 00:21:43.990
would never contemplate
advocating its destruction.

00:21:43.990 --> 00:21:46.990
The electoral college was
set up as a reflection

00:21:46.990 --> 00:21:50.230
of our republic, as a guard
against pure majority rule.

00:21:50.230 --> 00:21:53.710
And those who are now pushing
a change or an end to it

00:21:53.710 --> 00:21:57.940
are not doing so because they
believe in a better country.

00:21:57.940 --> 00:21:59.770
Come on.

00:21:59.770 --> 00:22:03.670
They're doing so for
pure partisan power.

00:22:03.670 --> 00:22:08.800
They want to be in control and
then stay in control forever.

00:22:08.800 --> 00:22:10.720
And these political
actors understand

00:22:10.720 --> 00:22:14.590
that socialist policies are a
further avenue for obtaining

00:22:14.590 --> 00:22:16.750
and keeping that power.

00:22:16.750 --> 00:22:19.900
Progressives can say that
they're interested in fairness,

00:22:19.900 --> 00:22:23.290
and health care for all,
and better education.

00:22:23.290 --> 00:22:26.650
Maybe that is what
motivates them to some part.

00:22:26.650 --> 00:22:28.450
But here's the thing.

00:22:28.450 --> 00:22:31.210
They also know that those
policies provide them

00:22:31.210 --> 00:22:34.030
extraordinary new
political power

00:22:34.030 --> 00:22:37.990
and control over
speech, over assembly,

00:22:37.990 --> 00:22:41.110
over money, over elections.

00:22:41.110 --> 00:22:43.870
Giving government
control over health care

00:22:43.870 --> 00:22:46.810
literally means giving
government control

00:22:46.810 --> 00:22:49.420
over life and death.

00:22:49.420 --> 00:22:51.880
Giving government
control over energy

00:22:51.880 --> 00:22:54.880
gives government control
over what people drive,

00:22:54.880 --> 00:22:58.240
what jobs they can have,
where they can travel,

00:22:58.240 --> 00:22:59.560
and what food they can eat.

00:22:59.560 --> 00:23:01.060
And if you don't
believe me, go look

00:23:01.060 --> 00:23:03.820
at the text of the Green
New Deal, in which they're

00:23:03.820 --> 00:23:07.090
honest about the decisions
that they intend to take

00:23:07.090 --> 00:23:09.760
on behalf of the electorate.

00:23:09.760 --> 00:23:13.600
They know from history
that these schemes do not

00:23:13.600 --> 00:23:16.400
lead to more equality
or to a better world.

00:23:16.400 --> 00:23:19.750
So we have to assume in the
end that a big part of this

00:23:19.750 --> 00:23:23.260
is instead about obtaining
raw political power,

00:23:23.260 --> 00:23:26.310
taking it and keeping it.

00:23:26.310 --> 00:23:28.990
And in the process,
they're taking advantage

00:23:28.990 --> 00:23:30.940
of those millions
of young Americans

00:23:30.940 --> 00:23:35.080
who have experienced those
structural shifts I mentioned,

00:23:35.080 --> 00:23:37.210
young Americans
who are demoralized

00:23:37.210 --> 00:23:41.020
by a global economy,
struggling at times for jobs,

00:23:41.020 --> 00:23:45.250
overloaded with student
debt, unable to buy a home.

00:23:45.250 --> 00:23:48.190
They are pandering to
those young Americans'

00:23:48.190 --> 00:23:50.310
basest instincts.

00:23:50.310 --> 00:23:51.940
The world isn't fair.

00:23:51.940 --> 00:23:53.520
It's stacked against you.

00:23:53.520 --> 00:23:56.160
The rich are keeping you down.

00:23:56.160 --> 00:23:57.630
I'll pay for your health care.

00:23:57.630 --> 00:23:59.400
I'll pay for your education.

00:23:59.400 --> 00:24:01.350
I'll penalize those
who would charge you

00:24:01.350 --> 00:24:06.820
for a mortgage, or a credit
card, or a prescription drug.

00:24:06.820 --> 00:24:08.820
These politicians are
not lifting them up.

00:24:08.820 --> 00:24:11.280
They're not inspiring
them to do better.

00:24:11.280 --> 00:24:15.730
They are capitalizing on fears,
and weaknesses, and worries.

00:24:15.730 --> 00:24:18.960
This is a failure of
leadership by people

00:24:18.960 --> 00:24:22.350
who know better, who have
an obligation to acknowledge

00:24:22.350 --> 00:24:25.440
history, to celebrate
US successes,

00:24:25.440 --> 00:24:29.550
and to use their power with
humility and restraint.

00:24:29.550 --> 00:24:32.650
So that's the Democrats.

00:24:32.650 --> 00:24:35.520
But I would point out that
the failure isn't just

00:24:35.520 --> 00:24:37.740
on the liberal side.

00:24:37.740 --> 00:24:43.260
This opportunity to prey
exists because too many of us

00:24:43.260 --> 00:24:46.260
on the conservative side have
not done a good enough job

00:24:46.260 --> 00:24:49.350
at combating it when
we simply haven't.

00:24:49.350 --> 00:24:50.520
We have to acknowledge that.

00:24:50.520 --> 00:24:52.885
[APPLAUSE]

00:24:55.730 --> 00:24:58.850
Everyone, young and
old, needs principles

00:24:58.850 --> 00:25:01.280
around which to
organize their lives.

00:25:01.280 --> 00:25:05.060
Young Americans are every day
hearing the socialist call.

00:25:05.060 --> 00:25:07.250
As I mentioned, your
life is too tough.

00:25:07.250 --> 00:25:09.440
The deck is stacked against you.

00:25:09.440 --> 00:25:11.480
You need us to lift you up.

00:25:11.480 --> 00:25:13.490
Government can do that.

00:25:13.490 --> 00:25:16.160
And they are not hearing
a compelling message

00:25:16.160 --> 00:25:17.630
to the contrary.

00:25:17.630 --> 00:25:20.150
Conservatives, and in
particular the elected members

00:25:20.150 --> 00:25:24.170
of the Republican Party,
are failing in that regard.

00:25:24.170 --> 00:25:27.560
And the biggest failure is
simply one of messaging.

00:25:27.560 --> 00:25:31.820
And it's a failure born, I
would wager, from conceit.

00:25:31.820 --> 00:25:34.280
Here's the problem
with conservatives.

00:25:34.280 --> 00:25:36.590
We innately
understand the beauty

00:25:36.590 --> 00:25:39.410
of free markets and
free people, so much so

00:25:39.410 --> 00:25:42.328
that we can't imagine
that nobody else does.

00:25:42.328 --> 00:25:44.120
And we don't think we
need to take the time

00:25:44.120 --> 00:25:47.240
to explain it to them.

00:25:47.240 --> 00:25:49.090
And sometimes, by the
way, conservatives

00:25:49.090 --> 00:25:52.030
are understandably further
discouraged by the need

00:25:52.030 --> 00:25:56.830
to tackle what are very complex
subjects and the world in which

00:25:56.830 --> 00:25:59.410
we live of 30-second
sound bites.

00:25:59.410 --> 00:26:01.180
That's a hard thing to do.

00:26:01.180 --> 00:26:04.000
It is, after all, much
easier for Progressives

00:26:04.000 --> 00:26:06.610
to get those democratic
presidential candidates calling

00:26:06.610 --> 00:26:09.140
for the abolition of
the electoral college.

00:26:09.140 --> 00:26:11.230
They have a very simple message.

00:26:11.230 --> 00:26:14.030
It's not fair.

00:26:14.030 --> 00:26:17.180
A majority of Americans
voted for Hillary Clinton.

00:26:17.180 --> 00:26:20.720
She ought to be president.

00:26:20.720 --> 00:26:24.590
When was the last time you heard
a prominent conservative leader

00:26:24.590 --> 00:26:28.220
take the time to explain why
the electoral college matters

00:26:28.220 --> 00:26:32.090
so much, its history
and its principles,

00:26:32.090 --> 00:26:34.670
to explain the
importance of guarding

00:26:34.670 --> 00:26:38.180
against pure democratic
majorities, the importance

00:26:38.180 --> 00:26:40.580
of encouraging
coalition building

00:26:40.580 --> 00:26:46.520
and nationwide campaigning, the
importance of us as a republic?

00:26:46.520 --> 00:26:48.410
Would anyone like
to actually hazard

00:26:48.410 --> 00:26:51.170
a guess of how many young
Americans under the age of 30

00:26:51.170 --> 00:26:55.540
even understand how the
electoral college works?

00:26:55.540 --> 00:27:00.270
Raise your hand if you
think it's like 10%.

00:27:00.270 --> 00:27:02.830
That's an extraordinary failing.

00:27:02.830 --> 00:27:04.380
They don't.

00:27:04.380 --> 00:27:06.270
And, by the way, those
who do might entirely

00:27:06.270 --> 00:27:07.770
be here on this campus.

00:27:07.770 --> 00:27:09.246
[LAUGHTER]

00:27:11.710 --> 00:27:14.340
Let me give you a
working world example

00:27:14.340 --> 00:27:17.430
of how this failure
works in practice

00:27:17.430 --> 00:27:19.740
and how this
failure also greases

00:27:19.740 --> 00:27:22.540
the wheels for socialism.

00:27:22.540 --> 00:27:24.750
I remember a particularly
brilliant senator

00:27:24.750 --> 00:27:27.720
by the name of Tom
Coburn from Oklahoma.

00:27:27.720 --> 00:27:30.060
Some of you might know him.

00:27:30.060 --> 00:27:32.520
He was an obstetrician by trade.

00:27:32.520 --> 00:27:36.030
And he was a true what I call
citizen legislator, meaning

00:27:36.030 --> 00:27:40.200
he came relatively briefly
to Washington to help better

00:27:40.200 --> 00:27:43.530
his country and to benefit
by sharing his knowledge,

00:27:43.530 --> 00:27:46.513
in particular, his knowledge
of the health care system.

00:27:46.513 --> 00:27:48.930
And after he spent a little
bit of time there, guess what?

00:27:48.930 --> 00:27:52.320
He left and went
back to his home.

00:27:52.320 --> 00:27:54.750
Senator Coburn spent
years exhorting

00:27:54.750 --> 00:27:56.790
his Republican colleagues
to make themselves

00:27:56.790 --> 00:27:59.850
knowledgeable on the
creeping costs and growing

00:27:59.850 --> 00:28:02.220
problems in the health sector--

00:28:02.220 --> 00:28:04.530
what was driving it,
just how many Americans

00:28:04.530 --> 00:28:08.460
were getting angry over it,
what the response should be.

00:28:08.460 --> 00:28:11.400
And his colleagues by
and large ignored him

00:28:11.400 --> 00:28:13.140
on the Republican side.

00:28:13.140 --> 00:28:16.380
He was told that health care
markets were complicated,

00:28:16.380 --> 00:28:19.260
the politics were
complicated, Republicans

00:28:19.260 --> 00:28:24.030
risked alienating voters if
they pushed the wrong policy.

00:28:24.030 --> 00:28:25.920
Senator Coburn and
several of his colleagues

00:28:25.920 --> 00:28:28.350
actually developed a
model health care reform

00:28:28.350 --> 00:28:30.750
based on free market
principles, but none

00:28:30.750 --> 00:28:32.280
of the Republicans
in his conference

00:28:32.280 --> 00:28:34.810
could agree to all of
the different elements.

00:28:34.810 --> 00:28:37.380
So nobody in the
end did anything,

00:28:37.380 --> 00:28:39.870
right up until the point
that an Illinois senator

00:28:39.870 --> 00:28:43.380
named Barack Obama
made fixing health care

00:28:43.380 --> 00:28:47.310
the centerpiece of his
2008 presidential campaign.

00:28:47.310 --> 00:28:48.840
And he had a lot
of Americans who

00:28:48.840 --> 00:28:51.270
were eager to hear his ideas
because they were fed up

00:28:51.270 --> 00:28:53.310
with the status quo.

00:28:53.310 --> 00:28:55.170
And he won on it,
and he pushed through

00:28:55.170 --> 00:28:58.830
on the barest of
majorities, Obamacare.

00:28:58.830 --> 00:29:01.560
Obamacare has since
performed exactly

00:29:01.560 --> 00:29:04.080
as conservatives
predicted it would--

00:29:04.080 --> 00:29:08.400
driving costs up even further,
disrupting care, undermining

00:29:08.400 --> 00:29:11.370
innovation, and making
health care unattainable

00:29:11.370 --> 00:29:16.470
for significant numbers
of Americans, so much so

00:29:16.470 --> 00:29:19.590
that we now have Democrats
saying that the only way to fix

00:29:19.590 --> 00:29:23.190
these problems, the
problems that they created,

00:29:23.190 --> 00:29:26.070
is to socialize medicine
and to put government

00:29:26.070 --> 00:29:28.263
in control of all of it.

00:29:28.263 --> 00:29:29.930
And Republicans are
doing the same thing

00:29:29.930 --> 00:29:32.250
they were doing a decade ago.

00:29:32.250 --> 00:29:34.940
They're decrying those
democratic plans.

00:29:34.940 --> 00:29:39.740
But even today-- and let me
stress this-- even today,

00:29:39.740 --> 00:29:43.430
they do not have
a unified reform

00:29:43.430 --> 00:29:50.270
in response, 15, 20 years
later, not with one voice

00:29:50.270 --> 00:29:51.860
and not with one vision.

00:29:51.860 --> 00:29:53.510
And so, by the
way, if you're one

00:29:53.510 --> 00:29:56.360
of those millions of
young Americans out there,

00:29:56.360 --> 00:29:59.450
it makes the choice in
this election very simple.

00:29:59.450 --> 00:30:02.420
You can vote for Republicans
and get the status quo, which

00:30:02.420 --> 00:30:05.690
nobody is very happy with,
or you can vote for Democrats

00:30:05.690 --> 00:30:07.380
and get free health care.

00:30:07.380 --> 00:30:09.470
Which would you
choose, given what's

00:30:09.470 --> 00:30:13.190
been told to you out
there in this environment?

00:30:13.190 --> 00:30:16.210
So this too is a
failure of leadership,

00:30:16.210 --> 00:30:19.030
by a group of elected leaders
who need to do more than just

00:30:19.030 --> 00:30:20.980
complain about the
other side, who

00:30:20.980 --> 00:30:24.040
need to have the passion
of their convictions

00:30:24.040 --> 00:30:26.530
and the mindset that
it is their obligation

00:30:26.530 --> 00:30:29.380
to teach, and explain,
and boldly tackle

00:30:29.380 --> 00:30:32.200
even complicated
subjects, and moreover

00:30:32.200 --> 00:30:35.800
who need to start coming up
with a strategic plan for how

00:30:35.800 --> 00:30:39.520
to break through the
stranglehold of the two

00:30:39.520 --> 00:30:41.530
other institutions
that are doing

00:30:41.530 --> 00:30:45.430
so much damage to our country,
who are also most directly

00:30:45.430 --> 00:30:48.070
and daily failing
our young Americans--

00:30:48.070 --> 00:30:52.600
the media and the
education complex.

00:30:52.600 --> 00:30:57.310
Our press is currently overdue
for singular criticism.

00:30:57.310 --> 00:31:00.843
For decades now the media
has been moving to the left.

00:31:00.843 --> 00:31:02.260
And this, I believe,
is a function

00:31:02.260 --> 00:31:05.500
of a growing elitism in
the media profession, one

00:31:05.500 --> 00:31:09.430
that's stomped out
diversity of viewpoint.

00:31:09.430 --> 00:31:13.190
Long gone are the days when
a farm boy could put himself

00:31:13.190 --> 00:31:16.150
through a community college,
start as a cub reporter

00:31:16.150 --> 00:31:18.850
at a local paper, and
one day find himself

00:31:18.850 --> 00:31:21.550
on a desk at The New York Times.

00:31:21.550 --> 00:31:24.220
Increasingly the reporters
who shape our daily narrative

00:31:24.220 --> 00:31:26.710
from these lofty
media purchases all

00:31:26.710 --> 00:31:29.230
come from the same
left-of-center communities,

00:31:29.230 --> 00:31:32.140
all went to the same
left-of-center colleges,

00:31:32.140 --> 00:31:34.860
all have the same
left-of-center views.

00:31:34.860 --> 00:31:39.070
By the way, I grew up in a
logging community in Oregon.

00:31:39.070 --> 00:31:41.800
I don't know a single
person who lives and works

00:31:41.800 --> 00:31:45.490
in journalism today who has even
a remotely similar experience

00:31:45.490 --> 00:31:46.630
to mine.

00:31:46.630 --> 00:31:49.540
In fact, they actually wonder
if we still have electricity

00:31:49.540 --> 00:31:53.080
in the places where I grew up.

00:31:53.080 --> 00:31:56.470
They're so divorced from
anything outside of New York

00:31:56.470 --> 00:31:57.160
or Washington.

00:31:57.160 --> 00:31:59.060
It's something to behold.

00:31:59.060 --> 00:32:01.930
And meanwhile, the
old guard editors

00:32:01.930 --> 00:32:05.140
who used to exist to
scrupulously stamp out

00:32:05.140 --> 00:32:08.890
hints of bias are all
things of the past.

00:32:08.890 --> 00:32:11.710
And one result is that most
of the mainstream media today

00:32:11.710 --> 00:32:16.960
actively campaigns against
capitalism and free markets.

00:32:16.960 --> 00:32:20.350
In 2018, researchers from
the Arizona State University

00:32:20.350 --> 00:32:23.360
and Texas A&amp;M
University surveyed

00:32:23.360 --> 00:32:28.490
462 financial journalists,
OK, financial journalists.

00:32:28.490 --> 00:32:31.210
These are the people who
have a special understanding

00:32:31.210 --> 00:32:35.223
of markets, finance,
corporations, trade.

00:32:35.223 --> 00:32:36.640
These are the
people who therefore

00:32:36.640 --> 00:32:39.460
ought to have the most
understanding of the virtues

00:32:39.460 --> 00:32:42.370
of capitalism, in
particular, by comparison

00:32:42.370 --> 00:32:47.630
to any other system the world,
ever, in all of history.

00:32:47.630 --> 00:32:49.180
And yet, the
researchers found that

00:32:49.180 --> 00:32:53.530
of these more than 450
journalists, nearly 60%

00:32:53.530 --> 00:32:57.430
admitted to being very
liberal or somewhat liberal.

00:32:57.430 --> 00:33:01.630
Another 37% described
themselves as moderate.

00:33:01.630 --> 00:33:08.610
And precisely 4.4% said they
leaned right of center, 4.4%.

00:33:12.470 --> 00:33:15.140
This is why the
mainstream media uniformly

00:33:15.140 --> 00:33:18.170
writes stories that are
hostile to tax cuts,

00:33:18.170 --> 00:33:21.140
to deregulation, to
labor market freedom,

00:33:21.140 --> 00:33:24.560
to health care freedom,
and to political campaign

00:33:24.560 --> 00:33:27.780
spending, which, by the way,
is a form of free speech.

00:33:27.780 --> 00:33:30.950
It's why they're also hostile
to corporations and businesses

00:33:30.950 --> 00:33:34.180
that employ tens of
millions of Americans.

00:33:34.180 --> 00:33:35.930
If you are a
millennial and you take

00:33:35.930 --> 00:33:38.720
an even occasional
interest in the news,

00:33:38.720 --> 00:33:43.650
this is the unrelenting message
that you are sent every day.

00:33:43.650 --> 00:33:46.550
In addition to this, you are
provided a never-ending stream

00:33:46.550 --> 00:33:49.040
of stories about
vulnerable communities

00:33:49.040 --> 00:33:50.900
who are unable to
help themselves

00:33:50.900 --> 00:33:53.120
without the aid of government.

00:33:53.120 --> 00:33:56.480
You also read fawning coverage
of socialist proposals

00:33:56.480 --> 00:33:58.290
offered by democratic
candidates,

00:33:58.290 --> 00:34:01.280
whether it be Medicare for
All or the Green New Deal,

00:34:01.280 --> 00:34:04.650
free college, the
forgiveness of student debt.

00:34:04.650 --> 00:34:06.420
And these stories are also--

00:34:06.420 --> 00:34:08.340
and I think this is important--

00:34:08.340 --> 00:34:12.300
not just fawning, but bereft
of any critical analysis

00:34:12.300 --> 00:34:15.090
of the failings of these
systems, failings that

00:34:15.090 --> 00:34:18.900
have already been demonstrably
proven in other countries

00:34:18.900 --> 00:34:21.300
that have adopted
similar proposals.

00:34:21.300 --> 00:34:22.889
We never-- when
was the last time

00:34:22.889 --> 00:34:25.980
you read a story about a Bernie
Sanders or Elizabeth Warren

00:34:25.980 --> 00:34:28.980
plan for Medicare for All
that mentioned the waiting

00:34:28.980 --> 00:34:31.949
lines or death panels that
accompany Britain's National

00:34:31.949 --> 00:34:34.020
Health Service?

00:34:34.020 --> 00:34:37.440
We hear nothing about the
collapse of medical innovation

00:34:37.440 --> 00:34:40.440
in other countries that have
instituted price controls.

00:34:40.440 --> 00:34:42.690
We hear nothing about the
mess that is currently

00:34:42.690 --> 00:34:45.690
the energy market in
places like Germany

00:34:45.690 --> 00:34:47.190
that have embraced
renewable energy

00:34:47.190 --> 00:34:49.440
programs of the
very type now being

00:34:49.440 --> 00:34:51.810
pushed by the American left.

00:34:51.810 --> 00:34:54.449
This amounts-- and I am
saying this as a journalist--

00:34:54.449 --> 00:34:57.870
to professional malpractice.

00:34:57.870 --> 00:34:59.985
But it explains,
yes, it is awful.

00:34:59.985 --> 00:35:01.560
[APPLAUSE]

00:35:01.560 --> 00:35:03.790
It is not journalism anymore.

00:35:03.790 --> 00:35:05.430
This is not journalism.

00:35:05.430 --> 00:35:08.040
But it explains why so
many young people are

00:35:08.040 --> 00:35:10.710
unaware of economic realities.

00:35:10.710 --> 00:35:14.220
Our press corps is failing them.

00:35:14.220 --> 00:35:16.470
And I would just
point out, those

00:35:16.470 --> 00:35:18.150
are just the factual questions.

00:35:18.150 --> 00:35:21.540
They are failing the country in
an even bigger and more scary

00:35:21.540 --> 00:35:25.080
way than just
economic illiteracy.

00:35:25.080 --> 00:35:27.210
Most socialist regimes
are accompanied

00:35:27.210 --> 00:35:30.750
by autocratic or
totalitarian tendencies.

00:35:30.750 --> 00:35:34.410
And that is because socialism,
as an economic project,

00:35:34.410 --> 00:35:36.660
is imposed from the top down.

00:35:36.660 --> 00:35:38.820
The more power you
have at the top,

00:35:38.820 --> 00:35:42.840
the easier it is to force
people to implement your agenda.

00:35:42.840 --> 00:35:45.450
And the guard against
this has always

00:35:45.450 --> 00:35:48.210
been a vigilant and
free press, which

00:35:48.210 --> 00:35:51.060
exists to be skeptical
of government power,

00:35:51.060 --> 00:35:54.090
to be on guard against
government abuse.

00:35:54.090 --> 00:35:56.730
That press, in turn,
leads to a more educated,

00:35:56.730 --> 00:35:59.820
active citizenry that
rises up against real

00:35:59.820 --> 00:36:03.060
or even perceived
abuses of power.

00:36:03.060 --> 00:36:05.100
In the past three
years, our press

00:36:05.100 --> 00:36:09.600
has completely
abdicated that role.

00:36:09.600 --> 00:36:12.780
The press has gone
beyond mere bias

00:36:12.780 --> 00:36:18.000
to actively and overtly joining
one side in a partisan war.

00:36:18.000 --> 00:36:22.080
And so visceral is its hatred
of this current president

00:36:22.080 --> 00:36:27.300
that it has abandoned all of its
basic standards and functions.

00:36:27.300 --> 00:36:29.970
This is a press corps that
for the past three years

00:36:29.970 --> 00:36:33.750
has praised and justified
an FBI investigation

00:36:33.750 --> 00:36:36.560
into a presidential campaign.

00:36:36.560 --> 00:36:38.260
Imagine that.

00:36:38.260 --> 00:36:42.310
Jim Comey's FBI engaged
in Hoover-like behavior,

00:36:42.310 --> 00:36:45.160
surveilling members of
a political campaign,

00:36:45.160 --> 00:36:47.710
keeping secret memos
of his conversations

00:36:47.710 --> 00:36:50.380
with the president,
leaking them later to gin

00:36:50.380 --> 00:36:52.310
up a special counsel.

00:36:52.310 --> 00:36:55.570
Not only has the press
blessed this behavior,

00:36:55.570 --> 00:36:59.050
it has sat and served
as a willing scribe

00:36:59.050 --> 00:37:01.690
for the self-serving narratives
of those who were actually

00:37:01.690 --> 00:37:03.930
fired from their jobs.

00:37:03.930 --> 00:37:06.550
It's put them on
TV, serving them up

00:37:06.550 --> 00:37:10.900
as neutral analysts on these
topics, the very people who

00:37:10.900 --> 00:37:13.660
were at the center of one of
the greatest political scandals

00:37:13.660 --> 00:37:16.390
in modern times.

00:37:16.390 --> 00:37:18.160
When I was coming up
through journalism,

00:37:18.160 --> 00:37:21.820
you were taught to question
anything a government official

00:37:21.820 --> 00:37:24.430
told you, to look out for spin.

00:37:24.430 --> 00:37:29.200
These guys are acting
as the narrators

00:37:29.200 --> 00:37:34.950
of that former institution's
leadership positions.

00:37:34.950 --> 00:37:37.070
And there is a generation
of younger Americans who

00:37:37.070 --> 00:37:39.170
have been told for
three years as a result

00:37:39.170 --> 00:37:43.280
that there is nothing wrong
whatsoever with powerful law

00:37:43.280 --> 00:37:46.850
enforcement and intelligence
agencies secretly monitoring

00:37:46.850 --> 00:37:48.185
American political figures.

00:37:50.870 --> 00:37:53.330
We have a press corps
that these days routinely

00:37:53.330 --> 00:37:56.660
tells that younger generation
that it's OK for Americans

00:37:56.660 --> 00:38:00.080
to settle their political
differences via impeachment,

00:38:00.080 --> 00:38:03.110
for one party to overturn
the results of an election

00:38:03.110 --> 00:38:05.528
because they don't
like those results.

00:38:05.528 --> 00:38:07.820
We have a press corps that
tells the younger generation

00:38:07.820 --> 00:38:10.940
that it's OK to
ambush a Supreme Court

00:38:10.940 --> 00:38:14.720
nominee with uncorroborated
allegations of sexual assault

00:38:14.720 --> 00:38:19.490
and gang rape and to put
forward those allegations

00:38:19.490 --> 00:38:21.830
despite no evidence
and in contravention

00:38:21.830 --> 00:38:25.100
of all basic principles,
like due process

00:38:25.100 --> 00:38:28.892
or the standard of innocence
until proven guilty.

00:38:28.892 --> 00:38:30.350
We have a press
corps-- by the way,

00:38:30.350 --> 00:38:33.650
a press corps that exists
because of our Constitution

00:38:33.650 --> 00:38:35.330
and the First Amendment--

00:38:35.330 --> 00:38:37.160
that tells our
younger generation

00:38:37.160 --> 00:38:40.850
that it is right and
indeed good for campuses

00:38:40.850 --> 00:38:44.550
to have speech codes
and safe spaces.

00:38:44.550 --> 00:38:47.280
Our press corps says this.

00:38:47.280 --> 00:38:49.380
And in light of all
this, of the failure

00:38:49.380 --> 00:38:53.640
to report honestly on the
failings of socialist economics

00:38:53.640 --> 00:38:56.310
and of the daily
justification they put forward

00:38:56.310 --> 00:39:00.390
of abuses of power, how can
we ever expect that younger

00:39:00.390 --> 00:39:06.420
generation to have a dim view
of abuse of power or socialism?

00:39:06.420 --> 00:39:09.900
Especially too, and this
brings us to our last--

00:39:09.900 --> 00:39:11.620
this is really a glum
speech, isn't it?

00:39:11.620 --> 00:39:12.120
Sorry.

00:39:12.120 --> 00:39:13.812
[LAUGHTER]

00:39:15.630 --> 00:39:18.820
I have some optimistic
points at the end.

00:39:18.820 --> 00:39:19.950
OK.

00:39:19.950 --> 00:39:21.960
Especially too, given
the education they're

00:39:21.960 --> 00:39:24.990
receiving, OK, the important
part of the speech.

00:39:24.990 --> 00:39:26.280
It starts in grade school.

00:39:26.280 --> 00:39:28.680
And whether your complaint
is poor education,

00:39:28.680 --> 00:39:31.930
or incomplete education,
or biased education,

00:39:31.930 --> 00:39:33.720
the problem is the same.

00:39:33.720 --> 00:39:35.875
By the way, I have
kids at all levels

00:39:35.875 --> 00:39:38.250
of school-- high school, middle
school, and grade school.

00:39:38.250 --> 00:39:39.333
By the way, just shoot me.

00:39:39.333 --> 00:39:43.410
Having kids in three
different schools is awful.

00:39:43.410 --> 00:39:46.650
And I love the fact
that they all learn

00:39:46.650 --> 00:39:48.180
about our civil rights era.

00:39:48.180 --> 00:39:49.800
It's really important.

00:39:49.800 --> 00:39:52.830
But I think that many of us out
there would be equally grateful

00:39:52.830 --> 00:39:55.320
if our children were also
provided an equivalent

00:39:55.320 --> 00:40:00.630
amount of study about the
founding of this country

00:40:00.630 --> 00:40:03.780
or the horrific histories
of other countries,

00:40:03.780 --> 00:40:06.750
from the Soviet Union
to China, that followed

00:40:06.750 --> 00:40:08.970
socialist and communist pasts.

00:40:08.970 --> 00:40:11.010
And we would be
equally grateful if we

00:40:11.010 --> 00:40:13.230
had schools that managed
to teach even half

00:40:13.230 --> 00:40:16.170
of our children to
adequately read and write

00:40:16.170 --> 00:40:19.050
by the time they
leave high school.

00:40:19.050 --> 00:40:21.760
Because, by the way, that
is not the case today.

00:40:21.760 --> 00:40:24.510
And if we're going to talk about
our obligations of our younger

00:40:24.510 --> 00:40:27.840
generation to understand
the failings of socialism,

00:40:27.840 --> 00:40:31.320
children who cannot adequately
read or write cannot be

00:40:31.320 --> 00:40:33.870
expected to take reasoned
decisions about choices

00:40:33.870 --> 00:40:37.020
of economic systems.

00:40:37.020 --> 00:40:40.560
When I go out these
days and people ask me

00:40:40.560 --> 00:40:44.340
what I think is the biggest
issue facing this country,

00:40:44.340 --> 00:40:46.440
I used to be conflicted on that.

00:40:46.440 --> 00:40:47.520
I'm not anymore.

00:40:47.520 --> 00:40:50.910
And so my first answer is
always higher education.

00:40:50.910 --> 00:40:53.310
And it simply is.

00:40:53.310 --> 00:40:56.880
Even when I attended college,
which is really a long time ago

00:40:56.880 --> 00:40:59.670
now, our places
of higher learning

00:40:59.670 --> 00:41:03.240
were already straying away
from the Western canon,

00:41:03.240 --> 00:41:04.380
from the great books.

00:41:04.380 --> 00:41:08.070
They were becoming captive
to grievance politics.

00:41:08.070 --> 00:41:10.880
So even when I went to college
at Princeton University,

00:41:10.880 --> 00:41:12.630
in my particular
course of study--

00:41:12.630 --> 00:41:16.020
and we were one of the first
years that had to do this--

00:41:16.020 --> 00:41:17.910
I was not allowed
to graduate unless I

00:41:17.910 --> 00:41:20.760
took what was called a
disadvantaged person's course.

00:41:23.540 --> 00:41:26.770
I'm not really sure if I'm
proud or embarrassed to say

00:41:26.770 --> 00:41:29.920
that it was the only requirement
I fulfilled at Princeton

00:41:29.920 --> 00:41:34.090
University as a film course.

00:41:34.090 --> 00:41:36.410
And my overriding
memory of that class,

00:41:36.410 --> 00:41:38.570
beyond getting to watch
Shaft, which, by the way,

00:41:38.570 --> 00:41:41.710
I did think was really
cool and very entertaining,

00:41:41.710 --> 00:41:46.220
was that we didn't learn
a great deal beyond anger.

00:41:46.220 --> 00:41:49.210
It was a course that was
designed to stoke discord,

00:41:49.210 --> 00:41:53.470
to dredge up animosity, to
pit student against student.

00:41:53.470 --> 00:41:55.900
And I really didn't
see the point.

00:41:55.900 --> 00:41:58.180
But that was at
least just one course

00:41:58.180 --> 00:42:01.060
that I took at university,
and I survived it.

00:42:01.060 --> 00:42:04.930
For most students today,
these ideological agendas

00:42:04.930 --> 00:42:07.750
are the bread and butter of
their college experience.

00:42:07.750 --> 00:42:10.630
They are an hourly experience.

00:42:10.630 --> 00:42:13.010
Our universities are
almost uniformly liberal,

00:42:13.010 --> 00:42:15.700
if not outright socialist
in their classes

00:42:15.700 --> 00:42:17.500
and in their teachings.

00:42:17.500 --> 00:42:19.300
Our professors, who
are hired and then

00:42:19.300 --> 00:42:22.450
protected under tenure,
lecture our students daily

00:42:22.450 --> 00:42:26.890
about an American society that
is racist, sexist, ageist,

00:42:26.890 --> 00:42:29.200
corporatist, and elitist.

00:42:29.200 --> 00:42:32.170
These kids are told that
the entire world is rigged

00:42:32.170 --> 00:42:35.890
against them and that the only
remedy for such a situation

00:42:35.890 --> 00:42:40.360
is more government that will
protect and equalize society.

00:42:40.360 --> 00:42:42.640
Three years ago, the
Econ Journal Watch

00:42:42.640 --> 00:42:45.870
did a study of more than
40 leading universities

00:42:45.870 --> 00:42:49.360
and questioned more
than 7,000 professors.

00:42:49.360 --> 00:42:52.030
Liberal professors outnumbered
conservative professors

00:42:52.030 --> 00:42:55.900
by a ratio of 12 to one.

00:42:55.900 --> 00:42:59.860
And by the way, the left finds
even that ratio intolerable.

00:42:59.860 --> 00:43:01.460
[LAUGHTER]

00:43:01.460 --> 00:43:03.542
In the last book I wrote,
The Intimidation Game,

00:43:03.542 --> 00:43:05.500
I wrote about an organization
which some of you

00:43:05.500 --> 00:43:09.630
may have heard about
called UnKoch My Campus.

00:43:09.630 --> 00:43:11.480
So you may know the
Koch Foundation,

00:43:11.480 --> 00:43:14.990
the two libertarian brothers--
one recently passed away--

00:43:14.990 --> 00:43:16.700
over the years
has made an effort

00:43:16.700 --> 00:43:19.520
to start up programs
of intellectual debate

00:43:19.520 --> 00:43:21.620
and free market on campuses.

00:43:21.620 --> 00:43:26.630
Intellectual debate, this is
what colleges exist to do.

00:43:26.630 --> 00:43:27.590
But no.

00:43:27.590 --> 00:43:29.300
There is a group of
Americans out there

00:43:29.300 --> 00:43:31.590
who do not want that debate.

00:43:31.590 --> 00:43:33.830
So when the foundation
chooses a university

00:43:33.830 --> 00:43:37.250
to start a program, the UnKoch
My Campus network, which

00:43:37.250 --> 00:43:39.860
is backed by labor unions,
and environmental groups,

00:43:39.860 --> 00:43:42.950
and liberal activists,
it organizes individuals

00:43:42.950 --> 00:43:45.680
to protest and to tar
any professors who

00:43:45.680 --> 00:43:47.480
might work at such a program.

00:43:47.480 --> 00:43:50.910
They file state FOIA requests
to get ahold of documents,

00:43:50.910 --> 00:43:53.060
and they try to embarrass
universities out

00:43:53.060 --> 00:43:56.870
of allowing these programs
to exist on their campuses.

00:43:56.870 --> 00:44:00.110
Now, I would point out,
you do not see such efforts

00:44:00.110 --> 00:44:04.100
to stop, say, billionaire
Tom Steyer from starting

00:44:04.100 --> 00:44:06.830
a program at Stanford,
as he did recently,

00:44:06.830 --> 00:44:10.740
that was entirely devoted to
the study of climate change.

00:44:10.740 --> 00:44:14.277
That kind of billionaire
underwriting is apparently OK.

00:44:14.277 --> 00:44:16.110
But when it comes to
billionaires supporting

00:44:16.110 --> 00:44:19.830
ideological diversity,
we have an entire group

00:44:19.830 --> 00:44:22.620
that is dedicated to making
sure students are not

00:44:22.620 --> 00:44:25.650
exposed to any thoughts
beyond those put forward

00:44:25.650 --> 00:44:29.460
by their socialist professors.

00:44:29.460 --> 00:44:31.610
I think the even bigger
problem, though--

00:44:31.610 --> 00:44:33.770
and again, this gets
back to what Dr. Arnn was

00:44:33.770 --> 00:44:35.780
saying-- kids are learning.

00:44:35.780 --> 00:44:39.170
We'll expect them to engage in
behavior that we do not always

00:44:39.170 --> 00:44:42.320
approve of, especially given
all of these influences

00:44:42.320 --> 00:44:44.280
around them all the time.

00:44:44.280 --> 00:44:47.660
So one of the biggest
concerns is the abdication

00:44:47.660 --> 00:44:52.740
of leadership among university
presidents, faculty, and staff.

00:44:52.740 --> 00:44:55.670
This is the real
problem, universities

00:44:55.670 --> 00:45:00.633
that coddle infantile, myopic,
or destructive behavior.

00:45:00.633 --> 00:45:03.050
In the immediate wake of the
election of President Trump--

00:45:03.050 --> 00:45:05.120
and I am not making this up--

00:45:05.120 --> 00:45:07.280
the University of
Kansas urged students

00:45:07.280 --> 00:45:11.250
to make use of therapy dogs.

00:45:11.250 --> 00:45:14.460
The University of Michigan
comforted its distraught

00:45:14.460 --> 00:45:17.370
students by handing out
coloring books and Play-Doh.

00:45:17.370 --> 00:45:19.050
[LAUGHTER]

00:45:19.890 --> 00:45:20.610
No joke.

00:45:20.610 --> 00:45:23.580
And I really hope Hillsdale does
not have a supply of Play-Doh

00:45:23.580 --> 00:45:27.090
anywhere on campus.

00:45:27.090 --> 00:45:29.430
Across the country,
students and even faculty

00:45:29.430 --> 00:45:32.100
have revolted against visiting
conservative speakers--

00:45:32.100 --> 00:45:35.700
staging demonstrations,
engaging in violence.

00:45:35.700 --> 00:45:37.650
By the way, the response
to such behavior

00:45:37.650 --> 00:45:40.860
ought to be a little bit like
the story we heard tonight.

00:45:40.860 --> 00:45:42.480
It ought to be to
remind students

00:45:42.480 --> 00:45:44.880
that they are at a
university to learn,

00:45:44.880 --> 00:45:48.780
that they are expected to
behave in a certain way,

00:45:48.780 --> 00:45:51.330
and that those who
disrupt or protest

00:45:51.330 --> 00:45:55.050
the noble pursuit of learning
will be asked to leave,

00:45:55.050 --> 00:45:58.810
to surrender their spot to
a more open-minded scholar.

00:45:58.810 --> 00:46:00.580
Instead, university
administrations

00:46:00.580 --> 00:46:03.880
bow to these demands, abandon
intellectual diversity,

00:46:03.880 --> 00:46:07.130
and turn a blind eye
to atrocious behavior.

00:46:07.130 --> 00:46:12.940
So again, how can we expect our
students to reject and abhor

00:46:12.940 --> 00:46:15.430
socialist and
authoritarian impulses when

00:46:15.430 --> 00:46:19.030
the leaders of their schools
are encouraging them to indulge

00:46:19.030 --> 00:46:22.450
in those impulses every day?

00:46:22.450 --> 00:46:26.620
Now, there are universities
that give us cause for hope,

00:46:26.620 --> 00:46:29.470
places like Hillsdale, or
places like, I'm actually

00:46:29.470 --> 00:46:31.540
happy to say, my alma
mater, Princeton.

00:46:31.540 --> 00:46:33.800
This might surprise you.

00:46:33.800 --> 00:46:36.370
There are two professors at
Princeton, Robbie George, who

00:46:36.370 --> 00:46:38.770
is a conservative,
and Cornel West, who

00:46:38.770 --> 00:46:42.040
is a liberal, who many
years ago on that campus

00:46:42.040 --> 00:46:45.290
teamed up to argue
jointly that universities

00:46:45.290 --> 00:46:49.810
must be a place for open debate
in a liberal arts education.

00:46:49.810 --> 00:46:52.330
And they have
routinely come together

00:46:52.330 --> 00:46:55.600
to press for civil debate
and true speaking on campus.

00:46:55.600 --> 00:46:57.910
And that alliance
has not only allowed

00:46:57.910 --> 00:47:00.250
a lot of really great
speakers to come to Princeton

00:47:00.250 --> 00:47:02.890
University, but it's also
allowed the university

00:47:02.890 --> 00:47:08.320
to feel competent enough to hire
more conservative professors

00:47:08.320 --> 00:47:12.280
and to give students on that
campus a beginning, at least,

00:47:12.280 --> 00:47:16.010
of ideological diversity
in their classrooms.

00:47:16.010 --> 00:47:18.010
We have to have this.

00:47:18.010 --> 00:47:20.650
When I went through school,
when many of us in this room

00:47:20.650 --> 00:47:23.260
went through school, we
had teachers and later

00:47:23.260 --> 00:47:27.990
professors on different sides
of the ideological spectrum.

00:47:27.990 --> 00:47:33.090
And it was crucial to us to
hear those opposing viewpoints.

00:47:33.090 --> 00:47:36.780
These days, our students
are only hearing one side,

00:47:36.780 --> 00:47:39.180
and it's the song of socialism.

00:47:39.180 --> 00:47:42.440
Moreover, I should
point out, we parents

00:47:42.440 --> 00:47:45.180
are actually paying for it.

00:47:45.180 --> 00:47:48.200
We're paying for them
to be indoctrinated.

00:47:48.200 --> 00:47:50.150
I know so many
parents these days

00:47:50.150 --> 00:47:53.510
who are simply terrified
of even sending their child

00:47:53.510 --> 00:47:55.790
to a center of higher learning.

00:47:55.790 --> 00:47:58.380
That's just wrong.

00:47:58.380 --> 00:48:01.330
So this is all by way
of saying, if we care,

00:48:01.330 --> 00:48:04.710
if we really are worried about
the resurgence of socialism

00:48:04.710 --> 00:48:08.670
in this country, we have
to look at the root causes.

00:48:08.670 --> 00:48:11.700
And we have to look
at the bad actors.

00:48:11.700 --> 00:48:15.210
Our country is always as
good as its information,

00:48:15.210 --> 00:48:18.210
and its teaching,
and its guardrails.

00:48:18.210 --> 00:48:23.310
And right now our information
is poor, our teaching is poor,

00:48:23.310 --> 00:48:26.620
and our guardrails
are bent and broken.

00:48:26.620 --> 00:48:29.520
We need an informative,
neutral press

00:48:29.520 --> 00:48:31.860
that is holding government
and political parties

00:48:31.860 --> 00:48:35.310
to one standard of
political accountability,

00:48:35.310 --> 00:48:37.410
no matter what side of
the aisle they are on,

00:48:37.410 --> 00:48:39.360
no double standards.

00:48:39.360 --> 00:48:42.150
We need an education
system that educates,

00:48:42.150 --> 00:48:44.280
that explains to our
children the founding

00:48:44.280 --> 00:48:46.830
principles of this
nation, and that

00:48:46.830 --> 00:48:49.500
embraces ideological diversity.

00:48:49.500 --> 00:48:53.610
And we need political parties
that look beyond raw power

00:48:53.610 --> 00:48:59.250
and that take the time to engage
in considered civic debate.

00:48:59.250 --> 00:49:00.980
So how do we do this?

00:49:00.980 --> 00:49:03.800
So this is the happy
part of the speech.

00:49:03.800 --> 00:49:06.710
I think the good news
is is that we can.

00:49:06.710 --> 00:49:07.820
We can.

00:49:07.820 --> 00:49:11.480
I agree with Dr. Arnn
earlier that we can do this.

00:49:11.480 --> 00:49:12.530
I'm a conservative.

00:49:12.530 --> 00:49:15.320
It means I am an
optimist by nature.

00:49:15.320 --> 00:49:17.450
And one thing that gives
me enormous optimism

00:49:17.450 --> 00:49:20.930
is that this generation
that we are discussing,

00:49:20.930 --> 00:49:23.510
the one that is currently
somewhat in thrall

00:49:23.510 --> 00:49:28.370
to socialism, it is
actually a perfect audience

00:49:28.370 --> 00:49:32.760
for conservative, libertarian,
and capitalist ideas.

00:49:32.760 --> 00:49:37.800
This is a generation that hates,
in theory, top-down control.

00:49:37.800 --> 00:49:41.150
They are skeptical of
most forms of power.

00:49:41.150 --> 00:49:42.920
This is a generation
that was grown up

00:49:42.920 --> 00:49:45.590
able to get an Uber
whenever they want it

00:49:45.590 --> 00:49:48.560
and find any information
at the touch of Google.

00:49:48.560 --> 00:49:51.500
They start businesses in
their parents' basements.

00:49:51.500 --> 00:49:53.450
They crowdsource
capital investment.

00:49:53.450 --> 00:49:56.510
They reject traditional
business structures,

00:49:56.510 --> 00:49:59.660
unlike old fogies like
me, who, by the way,

00:49:59.660 --> 00:50:02.950
has only held one
job in her lifetime

00:50:02.950 --> 00:50:05.390
and is now rounding
into her 25th year

00:50:05.390 --> 00:50:07.560
at The Wall Street Journal.

00:50:07.560 --> 00:50:08.060
[APPLAUSE]

00:50:08.060 --> 00:50:16.540
Well-- like I'm saying,
they wouldn't applaud.

00:50:16.540 --> 00:50:17.680
They switch jobs.

00:50:17.680 --> 00:50:19.240
They leverage employers.

00:50:19.240 --> 00:50:21.560
They're unafraid to take risks.

00:50:21.560 --> 00:50:25.810
These are the kids who are
and who should therefore

00:50:25.810 --> 00:50:28.060
reject promises
of government jobs

00:50:28.060 --> 00:50:31.180
and government-imposed
equality or government nirvana.

00:50:31.180 --> 00:50:33.880
All that they're really
lacking is the information

00:50:33.880 --> 00:50:37.990
and the experience to
connect those political dots.

00:50:37.990 --> 00:50:40.680
And the other
source of optimism?

00:50:40.680 --> 00:50:44.590
We, the people, still have
the power to change things.

00:50:44.590 --> 00:50:46.010
We really do.

00:50:46.010 --> 00:50:49.180
And we do so by
embracing, in my mind,

00:50:49.180 --> 00:50:54.750
one of those fundamental aspects
of capitalism, consumerism.

00:50:54.750 --> 00:50:56.760
Something I've always
loved about this country

00:50:56.760 --> 00:51:00.780
is just how demanding we are
as consumers, at least in some

00:51:00.780 --> 00:51:01.860
regards.

00:51:01.860 --> 00:51:05.370
I still delight when I go
to a diner and I watch some

00:51:05.370 --> 00:51:09.780
80-year-old guy take 20 minutes
to look at his bill and make

00:51:09.780 --> 00:51:13.140
sure no one charged him
$0.50 extra for the extra bit

00:51:13.140 --> 00:51:17.370
of bacon on the side, and then
painstakingly work out what

00:51:17.370 --> 00:51:21.660
the 15% tip is, and make sure
he doesn't give an extra penny.

00:51:21.660 --> 00:51:25.620
I love it when I go and I see
consumers in the returns line

00:51:25.620 --> 00:51:27.750
at store, and they're
rejecting the toaster that

00:51:27.750 --> 00:51:31.230
doesn't get quite hot
enough, or the blanket that

00:51:31.230 --> 00:51:34.850
wasn't as fuzzy as
was advertised on TV,

00:51:34.850 --> 00:51:37.950
or the kid's toy that
doesn't perform exactly

00:51:37.950 --> 00:51:40.300
as the manufacturers
said it would.

00:51:40.300 --> 00:51:43.740
And I think the trick for all
of us is being just as picky

00:51:43.740 --> 00:51:46.620
and just as demanding
in our accountability

00:51:46.620 --> 00:51:50.250
of our institutions
and politicians.

00:51:50.250 --> 00:51:53.130
We must, as a society,
hold government officials

00:51:53.130 --> 00:51:56.190
responsible for
abusing their powers,

00:51:56.190 --> 00:51:58.710
cheapening solemn
tools and institutions.

00:51:58.710 --> 00:52:01.860
And we must demand that our
elected leaders do a better

00:52:01.860 --> 00:52:05.970
job of articulating their
policies and their responses

00:52:05.970 --> 00:52:10.350
to socialism, explaining
not just why it's immoral,

00:52:10.350 --> 00:52:12.910
but why their own
answers are better.

00:52:12.910 --> 00:52:15.270
We also have to demand
more of our media.

00:52:15.270 --> 00:52:19.080
And when it fails to deliver
factuality, or history,

00:52:19.080 --> 00:52:22.440
or truth, or skepticism,
we have to kill it.

00:52:22.440 --> 00:52:23.400
Turn it off.

00:52:23.400 --> 00:52:24.960
Cancel the subscription.

00:52:24.960 --> 00:52:27.660
Turn off the channel, cut
the cord, whatever word

00:52:27.660 --> 00:52:28.860
you want to use these days.

00:52:28.860 --> 00:52:31.050
Don't pay for it.

00:52:31.050 --> 00:52:32.133
I think we must--

00:52:32.133 --> 00:52:34.050
and this is probably the
hardest part-- demand

00:52:34.050 --> 00:52:36.390
a lot more from our
colleges, and universities,

00:52:36.390 --> 00:52:38.970
and graduate schools, seriously.

00:52:38.970 --> 00:52:41.730
Many of you are alumni
at these schools.

00:52:41.730 --> 00:52:43.110
We must be involved.

00:52:43.110 --> 00:52:45.480
We have to take part in
those board elections,

00:52:45.480 --> 00:52:48.310
and those proxy resolutions,
and those votes.

00:52:48.310 --> 00:52:52.530
We have to refuse to contribute
until universities recommit

00:52:52.530 --> 00:52:54.570
to intellectual diversity.

00:52:54.570 --> 00:52:58.610
Call, and write letters,
and make clear the demands.

00:52:58.610 --> 00:53:00.360
Whenever I make these
sort of suggestions,

00:53:00.360 --> 00:53:03.030
I'm often told, yeah,
yeah, you're right.

00:53:03.030 --> 00:53:03.810
But you know what?

00:53:03.810 --> 00:53:06.390
It's just really hard,
so time consuming.

00:53:06.390 --> 00:53:07.510
I have a job.

00:53:07.510 --> 00:53:08.290
I have kids.

00:53:08.290 --> 00:53:10.320
I have family obligations--

00:53:10.320 --> 00:53:12.600
to which I always
like to respond,

00:53:12.600 --> 00:53:14.490
George Washington
and Thomas Jefferson

00:53:14.490 --> 00:53:15.900
were pretty busy guys, too.

00:53:15.900 --> 00:53:17.860
[LAUGHTER]

00:53:18.840 --> 00:53:20.410
So were all of the Founders.

00:53:20.410 --> 00:53:23.100
But they understood that
only through hard work

00:53:23.100 --> 00:53:28.120
would they create this
extraordinary republic of ours.

00:53:28.120 --> 00:53:30.600
And so I think as
we go ahead, we

00:53:30.600 --> 00:53:33.900
have to remember those
words of Benjamin Franklin--

00:53:33.900 --> 00:53:37.290
"It's a republic if
you can keep it."

00:53:37.290 --> 00:53:38.025
Thank you.

00:53:38.025 --> 00:53:40.280
[APPLAUSE]

00:54:02.280 --> 00:54:05.360
Thank you very much.

00:54:05.360 --> 00:54:06.770
You are an amazing audience.

00:54:10.870 --> 00:54:13.320
I just have to say,
by the way, you

00:54:13.320 --> 00:54:15.300
should be really proud
of Hillsdale for what

00:54:15.300 --> 00:54:17.880
it expects of its speakers.

00:54:17.880 --> 00:54:21.000
Whenever I go to give
a speech, I never

00:54:21.000 --> 00:54:23.460
just pull one out of a bottom
drawer and give the same one.

00:54:23.460 --> 00:54:25.350
I always feel
responsible to write one

00:54:25.350 --> 00:54:27.060
for each new group I go to.

00:54:27.060 --> 00:54:29.900
But when I come to
Hillsdale, I literally sweat.

00:54:29.900 --> 00:54:30.600
[LAUGHTER]

00:54:30.600 --> 00:54:32.873
I took three weeks
writing that speech

00:54:32.873 --> 00:54:34.290
because I'm like,
oh, man, this is

00:54:34.290 --> 00:54:37.425
such a sophisticated audience.

00:54:37.425 --> 00:54:39.780
And all these fancy people
are going to be there,

00:54:39.780 --> 00:54:42.060
and they're going to call
me out if I get it wrong,

00:54:42.060 --> 00:54:43.630
and they'll never
invite me back.

00:54:43.630 --> 00:54:44.650
So this was a hard one.

00:54:44.650 --> 00:54:46.294
Thank you.

00:54:46.294 --> 00:54:48.754
[APPLAUSE]

00:54:53.680 --> 00:54:55.760
TARYN MURPHY: Thank
you, Ms. Strassel.

00:54:55.760 --> 00:54:58.990
Ms. Strassel has agreed to
sign copies of her book,

00:54:58.990 --> 00:55:01.150
Resistance At All Costs--

00:55:01.150 --> 00:55:03.970
How Trump Haters Are
Breaking America.

00:55:03.970 --> 00:55:06.130
And that will be directly
following the lecture

00:55:06.130 --> 00:55:07.660
in the Searle lobby.

00:55:07.660 --> 00:55:09.422
And right now we have
time for some Q&amp;A.

00:55:09.422 --> 00:55:11.380
So if you have a question,
please make your way

00:55:11.380 --> 00:55:12.310
to the microphone.

00:55:15.003 --> 00:55:15.670
AUDIENCE: Hello.

00:55:15.670 --> 00:55:16.990
Can I call you Kim?

00:55:16.990 --> 00:55:17.560
KIMBERLEY STRASSEL:
Yes, you may.

00:55:17.560 --> 00:55:18.760
Only my mother
calls me Kimberley.

00:55:18.760 --> 00:55:19.593
AUDIENCE: Thank you.

00:55:19.593 --> 00:55:20.440
You did a great job.

00:55:20.440 --> 00:55:21.790
Here's my question.

00:55:21.790 --> 00:55:26.140
Let's all assume, and hope, and
pray that Trump wins in 2020.

00:55:26.140 --> 00:55:30.260
I have been worrying
about 2024--

00:55:30.260 --> 00:55:31.626
Kim for president?

00:55:31.626 --> 00:55:33.956
[LAUGHTER]

00:55:35.360 --> 00:55:36.680
KIMBERLEY STRASSEL: Thank you.

00:55:36.680 --> 00:55:37.400
No.

00:55:37.400 --> 00:55:38.660
It was funny.

00:55:38.660 --> 00:55:40.650
I got-- it was great.

00:55:40.650 --> 00:55:43.320
I got to sit with John Miller's
journalism students today.

00:55:43.320 --> 00:55:45.950
If you don't know John Miller,
he runs an amazing journalism

00:55:45.950 --> 00:55:46.550
project here.

00:55:46.550 --> 00:55:47.960
Everything here is amazing.

00:55:47.960 --> 00:55:49.820
He runs the amazing
journalism project.

00:55:49.820 --> 00:55:52.700
And someone asked me
how I made my choice

00:55:52.700 --> 00:55:54.350
of going into journalism.

00:55:54.350 --> 00:55:56.870
And I said, I was really
actually quite interested

00:55:56.870 --> 00:56:00.470
in the beginning in politics
and maybe working in Washington

00:56:00.470 --> 00:56:01.760
or even running for office.

00:56:01.760 --> 00:56:07.280
After covering politics for
one year, I said, never, never.

00:56:07.280 --> 00:56:11.110
It's a horrible life.

00:56:11.110 --> 00:56:13.790
Yeah, maybe something that
doesn't really require running.

00:56:16.990 --> 00:56:18.150
AUDIENCE: Hi.

00:56:18.150 --> 00:56:20.740
Thank you for your
talk, first of all.

00:56:20.740 --> 00:56:25.810
And it focused pretty
strictly on government.

00:56:25.810 --> 00:56:27.390
And I was wondering
your thoughts

00:56:27.390 --> 00:56:32.730
on measures that are
compatible with free market

00:56:32.730 --> 00:56:35.850
and free association,
like worker's cooperatives

00:56:35.850 --> 00:56:37.748
or consumer cooperatives.

00:56:37.748 --> 00:56:38.790
KIMBERLEY STRASSEL: Yeah.

00:56:38.790 --> 00:56:41.130
So I mean, I think
they can if they're

00:56:41.130 --> 00:56:45.880
centered around the
idea of free markets.

00:56:45.880 --> 00:56:47.130
I think some of the problems--

00:56:47.130 --> 00:56:48.960
I've seen some of
these in bigger cities,

00:56:48.960 --> 00:56:51.070
like New York City, et cetera.

00:56:51.070 --> 00:56:54.570
And they have a more
communistic feel,

00:56:54.570 --> 00:56:57.210
everyone donates their time.

00:56:57.210 --> 00:56:59.440
So I think that if you--

00:56:59.440 --> 00:57:02.052
we're seeing this, for instance,
in health care markets,

00:57:02.052 --> 00:57:03.760
in some of these
proposals, for instance,

00:57:03.760 --> 00:57:06.465
to have small employers
band together and sort

00:57:06.465 --> 00:57:11.460
of pool basic costs, but in a
way that lowers their overheads

00:57:11.460 --> 00:57:13.590
and allows them to keep
more of their profits,

00:57:13.590 --> 00:57:15.250
and give more to their workers.

00:57:15.250 --> 00:57:16.480
These are great ideas.

00:57:16.480 --> 00:57:19.620
And I think there's an
enormous opportunity

00:57:19.620 --> 00:57:21.330
for younger Americans
who are part

00:57:21.330 --> 00:57:25.690
of this whole new
connected economy.

00:57:25.690 --> 00:57:29.230
Right now we're seeing versions
of it in Uber, et cetera.

00:57:29.230 --> 00:57:32.250
But there are ways to do
that in amazing new ways

00:57:32.250 --> 00:57:36.630
that could lower overall costs,
help consumers, but do so

00:57:36.630 --> 00:57:38.230
in a free market way.

00:57:38.230 --> 00:57:40.800
I find Uber really fascinating
because, of course,

00:57:40.800 --> 00:57:42.630
it's knocked up
against-- it was started

00:57:42.630 --> 00:57:44.550
by good-hearted liberals.

00:57:44.550 --> 00:57:48.210
And it ended up getting knocked
up against all these unions

00:57:48.210 --> 00:57:50.730
that are like, you can't
drive a bunch of drivers

00:57:50.730 --> 00:57:52.960
that aren't unionized.

00:57:52.960 --> 00:57:55.500
And Silicon Valley
has had to deal

00:57:55.500 --> 00:57:58.450
with that
philosophical question.

00:57:58.450 --> 00:58:01.890
But networked societies,
there's enormous opportunity

00:58:01.890 --> 00:58:05.258
for free market younger
Americans to embrace this world

00:58:05.258 --> 00:58:07.050
that a lot of us older
people didn't really

00:58:07.050 --> 00:58:10.710
grow up in and figure
out ways to use it to do

00:58:10.710 --> 00:58:12.360
amazing things with capitalism.

00:58:14.795 --> 00:58:15.920
AUDIENCE: Hi, Ms. Strassel.

00:58:15.920 --> 00:58:18.330
Thank you so much for your talk.

00:58:18.330 --> 00:58:20.370
I agreed with you on a
lot of things tonight.

00:58:20.370 --> 00:58:20.870
But--

00:58:20.870 --> 00:58:21.870
KIMBERLEY STRASSEL: But?

00:58:21.870 --> 00:58:24.740
AUDIENCE: One thing I'd like
to hear your thoughts on

00:58:24.740 --> 00:58:30.320
is, when you talk about
the Democratic Party,

00:58:30.320 --> 00:58:35.057
you assigned more
malevolent motives to them.

00:58:35.057 --> 00:58:36.140
KIMBERLEY STRASSEL: Blame?

00:58:36.140 --> 00:58:36.640
Yes.

00:58:36.640 --> 00:58:37.450
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:58:37.450 --> 00:58:42.980
Well, I was wondering, if
we start assigning motives

00:58:42.980 --> 00:58:46.220
to them of saying they're
actually in it for power

00:58:46.220 --> 00:58:50.930
and control rather than
taking them at face value,

00:58:50.930 --> 00:58:54.750
aren't we losing
any common ground

00:58:54.750 --> 00:58:57.500
we can have as soon as we start
assigning motives to people

00:58:57.500 --> 00:59:00.690
that they won't admit to?

00:59:00.690 --> 00:59:03.770
And I'm sure there are
some bad Democrats.

00:59:03.770 --> 00:59:04.920
I totally agree.

00:59:04.920 --> 00:59:08.360
But if we just start--

00:59:08.360 --> 00:59:13.310
if we lump them all
together as power hungry,

00:59:13.310 --> 00:59:14.403
don't we lose a lot?

00:59:14.403 --> 00:59:16.070
KIMBERLEY STRASSEL:
So look, by the way,

00:59:16.070 --> 00:59:17.510
I wouldn't lump
them all together.

00:59:17.510 --> 00:59:20.060
I didn't put AOC in
that category of people

00:59:20.060 --> 00:59:22.340
that I think is
necessarily power hungry.

00:59:22.340 --> 00:59:26.570
I think she's just
tragically misinformed.

00:59:26.570 --> 00:59:30.050
And I think she's
28 and hasn't really

00:59:30.050 --> 00:59:32.300
had a lot of world experience.

00:59:32.300 --> 00:59:37.040
And I think she honestly
and scarily believes

00:59:37.040 --> 00:59:39.680
that we could just get
rid of all the airplanes,

00:59:39.680 --> 00:59:43.460
and all the cow farts,
and it would all be good.

00:59:43.460 --> 00:59:44.570
I just don't think--

00:59:44.570 --> 00:59:47.090
but my complaint-- and I tried
to point it out in here--

00:59:47.090 --> 00:59:49.670
is Bernie Sanders knows better.

00:59:49.670 --> 00:59:52.970
And when he's asked a question
out there about Venezuela

00:59:52.970 --> 00:59:55.280
and says, don't you have
a problem with what's

00:59:55.280 --> 00:59:57.410
happening in Venezuela,
and the people that

00:59:57.410 --> 00:59:59.870
are being shot in the
street, and the people who

00:59:59.870 --> 01:00:03.110
are starving, he goes, it's
not a socialist country.

01:00:03.110 --> 01:00:04.790
Come on.

01:00:04.790 --> 01:00:06.450
He knows better.

01:00:06.450 --> 01:00:09.140
And so that's the kind
of dishonesty in politics

01:00:09.140 --> 01:00:10.490
that I don't like.

01:00:10.490 --> 01:00:11.940
And I don't see how you can be--

01:00:11.940 --> 01:00:12.440
I'm sorry.

01:00:12.440 --> 01:00:13.690
I'm speaking very freely here.

01:00:13.690 --> 01:00:16.370
But I don't see how you
can be a politician that

01:00:16.370 --> 01:00:19.970
say you believe in the
betterment of human beings

01:00:19.970 --> 01:00:24.930
with that experience
unfolding before your eyes

01:00:24.930 --> 01:00:27.340
and say that that is something--

01:00:27.340 --> 01:00:30.630
the policies you want to
implement here in this country,

01:00:30.630 --> 01:00:32.790
knowing that that
could be what happened.

01:00:32.790 --> 01:00:34.520
So I think that's disingenuous.

01:00:34.520 --> 01:00:36.530
And I think they need
to be called out on it.

01:00:36.530 --> 01:00:38.745
Maybe he's misinformed too.

01:00:38.745 --> 01:00:41.120
I just find it harder to
believe that you can be that age

01:00:41.120 --> 01:00:43.248
and be that misinformed.

01:00:43.248 --> 01:00:45.718
[APPLAUSE]

01:00:51.160 --> 01:00:53.060
But it's a good question,
and you're right.

01:00:53.060 --> 01:00:55.720
I think we need to have
some basic places where

01:00:55.720 --> 01:00:56.620
we get together.

01:00:56.620 --> 01:00:58.395
I do.

01:00:58.395 --> 01:00:59.020
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

01:00:59.020 --> 01:00:59.855
I've been wondering--

01:00:59.855 --> 01:01:01.397
KIMBERLEY STRASSEL:
Oh, on this side.

01:01:01.397 --> 01:01:01.990
AUDIENCE: Oh.

01:01:01.990 --> 01:01:03.520
I've been wondering
over the years--

01:01:03.520 --> 01:01:05.350
it seems as if we are--

01:01:05.350 --> 01:01:06.920
the West is committing suicide.

01:01:06.920 --> 01:01:11.680
So we had this AOC lady
with a New Green Deal.

01:01:11.680 --> 01:01:13.490
Somebody pointed out
how impractical it

01:01:13.490 --> 01:01:15.550
is, as you mentioned yourself.

01:01:15.550 --> 01:01:17.020
She said, who cares?

01:01:17.020 --> 01:01:19.480
It is the moral thing to do.

01:01:19.480 --> 01:01:21.910
What morality was
she referring to?

01:01:21.910 --> 01:01:28.590
What is the kind of morality
that the good guys, we, do not

01:01:28.590 --> 01:01:30.450
know how to take away from them?

01:01:30.450 --> 01:01:33.450
And in my opinion, it's
the morality of altruism.

01:01:33.450 --> 01:01:36.480
Man has no right to
live for his own sake.

01:01:36.480 --> 01:01:41.130
That is the underlying morality
that's destroying us now.

01:01:41.130 --> 01:01:41.630
Am I wrong?

01:01:41.630 --> 01:01:43.088
KIMBERLEY STRASSEL:
No, you're not.

01:01:43.088 --> 01:01:44.820
And that was sort
of what I was trying

01:01:44.820 --> 01:01:46.560
to respond to this one is--

01:01:46.560 --> 01:01:48.240
to this other question is--

01:01:48.240 --> 01:01:51.840
I mean, the education has
so failed our students

01:01:51.840 --> 01:01:54.120
that not only do they
not know the history

01:01:54.120 --> 01:01:56.710
and what has
happened in the past,

01:01:56.710 --> 01:01:58.350
but they've also
taught them to believe

01:01:58.350 --> 01:02:01.590
that I can say my
morality is right,

01:02:01.590 --> 01:02:03.280
everyone else's
morality is wrong,

01:02:03.280 --> 01:02:05.430
and I will impose my viewpoint--

01:02:05.430 --> 01:02:10.320
that somehow it is moral to
impose my viewpoint on you,

01:02:10.320 --> 01:02:12.090
no matter what else.

01:02:12.090 --> 01:02:13.920
I think that's astonishing.

01:02:13.920 --> 01:02:17.670
I think she hasn't-- again, I
think she just is a person that

01:02:17.670 --> 01:02:20.048
hasn't had a lot of--

01:02:20.048 --> 01:02:21.840
doesn't have a lot of
knowledge and doesn't

01:02:21.840 --> 01:02:24.030
have a lot of experience,
but unfortunately has

01:02:24.030 --> 01:02:27.210
a great deal of arrogance,
and unfortunately, worse,

01:02:27.210 --> 01:02:29.790
has a great deal of power.

01:02:29.790 --> 01:02:31.800
And that is very
disturbing to me.

01:02:31.800 --> 01:02:33.480
It's why I talk
about accountability.

01:02:33.480 --> 01:02:35.460
I thought one of the
most amazing things that

01:02:35.460 --> 01:02:38.820
was said this year
because it was so honest,

01:02:38.820 --> 01:02:41.160
and I don't even think
Nancy Pelosi realized

01:02:41.160 --> 01:02:44.340
she was being so honest, is
she was asked to say something

01:02:44.340 --> 01:02:45.030
about the squad.

01:02:45.030 --> 01:02:47.670
This was back before she
made nicey with them.

01:02:47.670 --> 01:02:50.250
And she was a little
bit mad at AOC.

01:02:50.250 --> 01:02:52.320
And she said, well,
AOC can say anything

01:02:52.320 --> 01:02:54.330
she wants because,
honestly, in her district,

01:02:54.330 --> 01:02:56.820
you could run a glass of
water with the letter D

01:02:56.820 --> 01:02:58.845
after the end, and
it would get elected.

01:02:58.845 --> 01:03:01.020
[LAUGHTER]

01:03:01.020 --> 01:03:02.850
And her point that
she was trying to make

01:03:02.850 --> 01:03:05.580
is that some Democrats come
from more competitive districts,

01:03:05.580 --> 01:03:07.880
and they can't
afford to be insane.

01:03:07.880 --> 01:03:09.640
[LAUGHTER]

01:03:09.640 --> 01:03:13.860
But in doing so, she exposed
one of the fundamental problems

01:03:13.860 --> 01:03:15.720
that I mentioned
in this speech, is

01:03:15.720 --> 01:03:17.310
that we have too
many politicians that

01:03:17.310 --> 01:03:20.070
are unaccountable
to the electorate.

01:03:20.070 --> 01:03:25.020
Do you know that every year
435 seats are up for election

01:03:25.020 --> 01:03:29.130
in the House of Representatives,
and about 40 of them

01:03:29.130 --> 01:03:30.180
are competitive?

01:03:30.180 --> 01:03:31.900
40.

01:03:31.900 --> 01:03:33.820
Everything else is
so gerrymandered

01:03:33.820 --> 01:03:35.770
that not one of those
people has to worry

01:03:35.770 --> 01:03:37.780
about their re-election.

01:03:37.780 --> 01:03:39.790
So of course that
encourages people

01:03:39.790 --> 01:03:42.540
to take extreme positions.

01:03:42.540 --> 01:03:46.000
Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez's
only concern

01:03:46.000 --> 01:03:49.202
was beating the Democrat
in her primary election.

01:03:49.202 --> 01:03:50.910
There was never any
question a Republican

01:03:50.910 --> 01:03:53.858
could have any possibility
of ever winning that seat.

01:03:53.858 --> 01:03:55.900
And so she wasn't challenged
on any of her views.

01:03:55.900 --> 01:03:58.180
And now she comes, and the
TV cameras are put on her,

01:03:58.180 --> 01:04:00.550
and she is suddenly
hugely influential.

01:04:00.550 --> 01:04:03.190
We can't allow our politics
to operate this way.

01:04:05.930 --> 01:04:06.430
Yes.

01:04:09.670 --> 01:04:10.620
AUDIENCE: Hi.

01:04:10.620 --> 01:04:13.970
I loved your lecture,
but I had a question.

01:04:13.970 --> 01:04:15.990
You were talking about
education and stuff,

01:04:15.990 --> 01:04:18.760
and intellectual diversity.

01:04:18.760 --> 01:04:20.520
And this is not
something I agree with.

01:04:20.520 --> 01:04:22.710
I'm just arguing
from the other side.

01:04:22.710 --> 01:04:27.630
A lot of people would
say that Hillsdale is not

01:04:27.630 --> 01:04:32.610
entirely neutral when it comes
to politics, and education,

01:04:32.610 --> 01:04:36.770
and the worldview we put out
to the students who come here.

01:04:36.770 --> 01:04:42.060
So what would you say makes
Hillsdale different and not

01:04:42.060 --> 01:04:44.640
just doing the same thing all
the other schools are doing,

01:04:44.640 --> 01:04:45.843
just flipped?

01:04:45.843 --> 01:04:47.760
KIMBERLEY STRASSEL: So
does Hillsdale-- here's

01:04:47.760 --> 01:04:48.180
the question.

01:04:48.180 --> 01:04:49.347
I'm actually really curious.

01:04:49.347 --> 01:04:54.158
Does Hillsdale prevent liberal
speakers from speaking here?

01:04:54.158 --> 01:04:54.700
AUDIENCE: No.

01:04:54.700 --> 01:04:55.060
KIMBERLEY STRASSEL: No?

01:04:55.060 --> 01:04:57.820
AUDIENCE: I mean, we don't
have that many of them come.

01:04:57.820 --> 01:04:58.862
KIMBERLEY STRASSEL: Yeah.

01:04:58.862 --> 01:05:00.877
But would it ever prevent it?

01:05:00.877 --> 01:05:01.960
AUDIENCE: I guess not, no.

01:05:01.960 --> 01:05:03.236
KIMBERLEY STRASSEL: No.

01:05:03.236 --> 01:05:05.800
And would you feel,
does it prevent

01:05:05.800 --> 01:05:08.740
forms of information coming?

01:05:08.740 --> 01:05:11.560
I think one of the things that's
out there is the whole world,

01:05:11.560 --> 01:05:15.580
every TV station is
surrounded by liberal politics

01:05:15.580 --> 01:05:17.650
and liberal-- and
presumably, tons of you

01:05:17.650 --> 01:05:18.880
see that on your phones.

01:05:18.880 --> 01:05:21.730
And nobody's taken
away your phones lately

01:05:21.730 --> 01:05:23.510
and things like that.

01:05:23.510 --> 01:05:24.010
No.

01:05:24.010 --> 01:05:26.650
I think, look, ideological
diversity is simply

01:05:26.650 --> 01:05:28.000
important as a concept.

01:05:28.000 --> 01:05:29.650
And I have no doubt--

01:05:29.650 --> 01:05:31.780
I mean, just the journalism
class I was at today,

01:05:31.780 --> 01:05:35.500
we talked about things on
left and right sides of--

01:05:35.500 --> 01:05:38.710
so that's the question,
is do you feel confident?

01:05:38.710 --> 01:05:40.210
I mean, I guess one
of the questions

01:05:40.210 --> 01:05:43.660
is, does anyone
here feel threatened

01:05:43.660 --> 01:05:48.260
about bringing up a question,
or a topic, or an idea?

01:05:48.260 --> 01:05:49.350
And I think that that--

01:05:49.350 --> 01:05:50.372
what's that?

01:05:50.372 --> 01:05:52.260
AUDIENCE: The boy in the shirt.

01:05:52.260 --> 01:05:54.010
KIMBERLEY STRASSEL:
The boy in the shirt--

01:05:54.010 --> 01:05:56.440
the boy in the shirt who came
out, and he did his thing.

01:05:56.440 --> 01:05:59.190
But does anyone actually feel
like they couldn't bring it up?

01:05:59.190 --> 01:06:01.800
And I think that that is one of
the problems that I hear from

01:06:01.800 --> 01:06:04.440
students at other universities,
is that they actually feel they

01:06:04.440 --> 01:06:07.200
cannot even raise a
question in class,

01:06:07.200 --> 01:06:10.590
that they could write a
paper that was opposite

01:06:10.590 --> 01:06:15.300
of their professor's views
and fail as a result of it,

01:06:15.300 --> 01:06:18.150
whereas I fundamentally don't
believe that that happens

01:06:18.150 --> 01:06:20.640
at this university because
this university does celebrate

01:06:20.640 --> 01:06:25.180
and believe in diversity
of thought, so.

01:06:25.180 --> 01:06:26.820
But it's a great question.

01:06:26.820 --> 01:06:27.820
TARYN MURPHY: Thank you.

01:06:27.820 --> 01:06:29.380
We have time for
one final question.

01:06:29.380 --> 01:06:31.110
KIMBERLEY STRASSEL: OK.

01:06:31.110 --> 01:06:34.300
AUDIENCE: Thank you very much
for coming out again today.

01:06:34.300 --> 01:06:37.500
I appreciate very much
how you ended your speech.

01:06:37.500 --> 01:06:40.050
I was thinking about
that specific comment

01:06:40.050 --> 01:06:43.980
as you were doing
your whole speech.

01:06:43.980 --> 01:06:46.220
As it relates to
one of the things

01:06:46.220 --> 01:06:47.940
that I was more
or less expecting

01:06:47.940 --> 01:06:51.287
you to touch on a little more,
I would even go further down

01:06:51.287 --> 01:06:53.370
the education realm into
the public school system,

01:06:53.370 --> 01:06:56.220
not only at the higher education
where I see a lot of issues

01:06:56.220 --> 01:06:59.860
going or taking place.

01:06:59.860 --> 01:07:01.638
When I try to
instruct my children,

01:07:01.638 --> 01:07:03.180
I always tell them
that there's going

01:07:03.180 --> 01:07:05.650
to be a person who
is an influencer,

01:07:05.650 --> 01:07:08.260
and there are those who
are easily influenced.

01:07:08.260 --> 01:07:10.833
As an influencer, it's OK to--

01:07:10.833 --> 01:07:12.250
you're going to
influence someone,

01:07:12.250 --> 01:07:14.380
or someone could be
influenced to do something

01:07:14.380 --> 01:07:16.930
good or something bad.

01:07:16.930 --> 01:07:18.940
As one who's easily
influenced, they

01:07:18.940 --> 01:07:23.420
could be influenced to do
what is good and what is bad.

01:07:23.420 --> 01:07:28.450
The question is knowing and
understanding what is good

01:07:28.450 --> 01:07:29.170
and what is bad.

01:07:29.170 --> 01:07:30.170
KIMBERLEY STRASSEL:
That's a good point.

01:07:30.170 --> 01:07:32.740
AUDIENCE: And so when I go
and try to talk to my kids

01:07:32.740 --> 01:07:36.170
about that, I try to make
that clear distinction.

01:07:36.170 --> 01:07:41.660
And when I address faith with
them, wherever God is removed--

01:07:41.660 --> 01:07:43.560
everything about God
tends to be removed,

01:07:43.560 --> 01:07:45.780
and we try to replace
that with policies.

01:07:45.780 --> 01:07:49.817
So how would you address and
bring that type of a topic

01:07:49.817 --> 01:07:51.775
up in some of your writings
or in conversations

01:07:51.775 --> 01:07:56.242
that you would have in
the journalistic essence?

01:07:56.242 --> 01:07:58.200
KIMBERLEY STRASSEL: You're
talking about faith?

01:07:58.200 --> 01:07:59.670
AUDIENCE: Yes, bringing that
up because you didn't really

01:07:59.670 --> 01:08:01.650
touch on faith as being
some of the issues

01:08:01.650 --> 01:08:05.690
to help guide students as
to what is right and wrong.

01:08:05.690 --> 01:08:08.670
And that tends to
be the core issue,

01:08:08.670 --> 01:08:11.220
right down from the early
development of children

01:08:11.220 --> 01:08:13.898
to the policymakers
that we have today.

01:08:13.898 --> 01:08:14.940
KIMBERLEY STRASSEL: Yeah.

01:08:14.940 --> 01:08:17.069
It is fundamentally a problem.

01:08:17.069 --> 01:08:19.410
And it starts, I think,
at the grade school

01:08:19.410 --> 01:08:20.880
and public education
level, where

01:08:20.880 --> 01:08:25.960
there are so many schools
that have excised any--

01:08:25.960 --> 01:08:29.670
we all know this, the public
schools that we no longer allow

01:08:29.670 --> 01:08:32.670
to have a Christmas
celebration at school

01:08:32.670 --> 01:08:35.550
or any sort of
reference to anything

01:08:35.550 --> 01:08:37.654
that involves faith whatsoever.

01:08:37.654 --> 01:08:39.029
So some of you
may not know this.

01:08:39.029 --> 01:08:40.390
I actually spend my time--

01:08:40.390 --> 01:08:42.960
I split my time between
Washington, DC and my home

01:08:42.960 --> 01:08:46.170
with my husband in Alaska.

01:08:46.170 --> 01:08:49.649
And we live in a very
conservative community

01:08:49.649 --> 01:08:50.470
in Alaska.

01:08:50.470 --> 01:08:54.630
And I was practically shocked
when I went to my first child's

01:08:54.630 --> 01:08:56.790
choir concert, and
the kids busted out

01:08:56.790 --> 01:09:00.529
in a rendition of "God Bless
America" in the public schools.

01:09:00.529 --> 01:09:01.680
And I was like, wow.

01:09:01.680 --> 01:09:05.069
I like this place a lot.

01:09:05.069 --> 01:09:06.330
But yeah.

01:09:06.330 --> 01:09:08.399
We have a situation,
and we all know this,

01:09:08.399 --> 01:09:12.300
where too much of society has
forgotten that the separation

01:09:12.300 --> 01:09:15.274
of church and state doesn't mean
that you cannot have a church.

01:09:15.274 --> 01:09:16.649
It means the
state's not supposed

01:09:16.649 --> 01:09:19.080
to impede on a church's work.

01:09:19.080 --> 01:09:23.760
And we need to re-inject that
into our culture and society.

01:09:23.760 --> 01:09:26.200
And look, I think there's
a couple of really,

01:09:26.200 --> 01:09:29.170
really uplifting things
that we're seeing out there.

01:09:29.170 --> 01:09:31.350
The Trump administration
and its campaigns

01:09:31.350 --> 01:09:35.460
on behalf of religious
freedom are extraordinary

01:09:35.460 --> 01:09:36.794
and something to celebrate.

01:09:36.794 --> 01:09:39.029
[APPLAUSE]

01:09:39.029 --> 01:09:41.162
And if you didn't see
this just the other day--

01:09:41.162 --> 01:09:42.870
it didn't get enough
attention-- but they

01:09:42.870 --> 01:09:46.470
are crafting a new rule to
get rid of a last-minute Obama

01:09:46.470 --> 01:09:50.189
prohibition against
faith-based adoptions

01:09:50.189 --> 01:09:54.870
out there, which has been
a terrible, terrible thing.

01:09:54.870 --> 01:09:56.790
So I think that that
is-- you're right.

01:09:56.790 --> 01:09:59.760
That has got to be part of
the discussion as well too.

01:09:59.760 --> 01:10:01.680
We have gotten so
many things backward

01:10:01.680 --> 01:10:04.800
in our Constitution
in recent years,

01:10:04.800 --> 01:10:08.562
people using, maligning,
and misinterpreting,

01:10:08.562 --> 01:10:10.770
and not understanding what
it was actually there for.

01:10:10.770 --> 01:10:13.708
That is one of the
worst examples of it,

01:10:13.708 --> 01:10:15.750
the idea that we need to--
now, the Supreme Court

01:10:15.750 --> 01:10:18.330
took an itsy, bitsy step
in the right direction

01:10:18.330 --> 01:10:21.600
with their Brandenburg cross
decision, which is very good.

01:10:21.600 --> 01:10:23.033
But they need to
go a lot further,

01:10:23.033 --> 01:10:24.450
and we need to
remind the country.

01:10:24.450 --> 01:10:26.867
And we're going to have to
look for institutions like that

01:10:26.867 --> 01:10:30.570
to lead the way, I
think, but also colleges

01:10:30.570 --> 01:10:34.512
and, again, our leaders, to talk
about this stuff more openly.

01:10:34.512 --> 01:10:35.220
But you're right.

01:10:35.220 --> 01:10:36.720
It's a fundamental
aspect of this,

01:10:36.720 --> 01:10:39.120
and I should've
mentioned it tonight.

01:10:39.120 --> 01:10:39.930
Thank you.

01:10:39.930 --> 01:10:40.930
TARYN MURPHY: Thank you.

01:10:40.930 --> 01:10:43.280
[APPLAUSE]

